WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.166 --> 00:00:02.602
Some scenes in this program have been reenacted,

00:00:02.902 --> 00:00:06.006
including the depiction of certain crime scene investigations.

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:16.916
A scientist tries to establish when a murder occurred.

00:00:19.352 --> 00:00:22.722
His only clues are insects collected from the body.

00:00:25.291 --> 00:00:29.596
Forensic investigators puzzle over a partial skeleton found in a forest.

00:00:30.397 --> 00:00:32.565
They have two pieces of evidence to work with,

00:00:32.999 --> 00:00:35.201
a skull and a wasp's nest.

00:00:38.471 --> 00:00:39.873
In a trash-filled house,

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:42.175
detectives find three people dead.

00:00:44.577 --> 00:00:46.279
Two of the victims are mummified.

00:00:48.048 --> 00:00:49.516
To solve the bizarre case,

00:00:49.849 --> 00:00:52.318
investigators rely on dead beetles.

00:00:54.087 --> 00:00:57.023
Insects may be the only witnesses to a crime,

00:00:57.824 --> 00:00:59.859
but bugs tell no tales.

00:01:01.194 --> 00:01:01.861
Or do they?

00:01:46.172 --> 00:01:48.741
Sun-kissed Hawaii is a vacationer's dream.

00:01:49.609 --> 00:01:50.109
Sand,

00:01:50.376 --> 00:01:50.876
surf,

00:01:50.977 --> 00:01:54.314
and balmy temperatures lure tourists by the thousands.

00:01:56.249 --> 00:01:59.452
But life in paradise can have a seamier side.

00:02:05.625 --> 00:02:07.727
In late December 1989,

00:02:07.927 --> 00:02:11.931
a search team scoured the forest near a small town on the island of Oahu.

00:02:18.638 --> 00:02:21.007
They were looking for clues in the disappearance of

00:02:21.541 --> 00:02:23.109
32-year-old Roxanne Tandall.

00:02:26.279 --> 00:02:30.016
Family members were concerned because Roxanne hadn't shown up for work,

00:02:30.383 --> 00:02:32.218
and no one had heard from her in days.

00:02:42.462 --> 00:02:43.997
Detectives Andrew Glushenko,

00:02:44.464 --> 00:02:45.064
Joe Self,

00:02:45.331 --> 00:02:48.635
and Rufus Kaukani received the call that she was missing.

00:02:51.337 --> 00:02:54.741
Relatives told police this wasn't Roxanne's normal behavior.

00:02:56.042 --> 00:02:56.809
If she was safe,

00:02:57.076 --> 00:02:58.745
she would have come home or called.

00:02:59.979 --> 00:03:02.081
They feared something terrible had happened.

00:03:04.217 --> 00:03:06.553
They were afraid Roxanne may have been killed.

00:03:13.393 --> 00:03:19.332
Neighbors believed that she had a violent quarrel with estranged husband Benjamin shortly before she disappeared.

00:03:23.303 --> 00:03:24.938
The couple were about to be divorced,

00:03:25.338 --> 00:03:27.840
with Roxanne gaining custody of their two sons.

00:03:29.242 --> 00:03:33.513
Neighbors heard loud noises and several thumps coming from Tandall's house.

00:03:34.948 --> 00:03:37.383
Suspicion quickly fell on Benjamin Tandall.

00:03:39.419 --> 00:03:43.056
Although Benjamin had appeared in person to report Roxanne's disappearance,

00:03:43.489 --> 00:03:47.060
Detective Kaukani thought Tandall's subsequent behavior was odd.

00:03:49.162 --> 00:03:58.004
What was kind of suspicious about the report that was made by the husband at the Kahuku Police Station is that the officer asked for an identification,

00:03:58.004 --> 00:03:59.005
a photograph of her,

00:03:59.339 --> 00:03:59.839
so they could,

00:03:59.872 --> 00:04:00.840
you know,
pass it around.

00:04:01.407 --> 00:04:03.843
Instead of taking a photograph and taking it back to the station,

00:04:04.244 --> 00:04:06.212
he picked up clothes and he went to Wailua,

00:04:06.279 --> 00:04:08.181
which is probably another 20 miles,

00:04:08.214 --> 00:04:09.015
15 miles away,

00:04:09.215 --> 00:04:10.049
to wash clothes.

00:04:10.817 --> 00:04:15.755
And that's kind of not indicative of somebody who's caring and concerned about a missing person.

00:04:17.657 --> 00:04:19.158
All right,

00:04:19.192 --> 00:04:21.894
Dave.
Suspicious that foul play might be involved,

00:04:22.195 --> 00:04:24.931
police obtained a warrant and searched Roxanne's home.

00:04:27.533 --> 00:04:28.735
If Roxanne was alive,

00:04:29.002 --> 00:04:31.504
perhaps they could find some clues to help locate her.

00:04:33.806 --> 00:04:35.808
If Benjamin had kidnapped or killed her,

00:04:36.242 --> 00:04:37.777
they needed evidence to prove it.

00:04:40.113 --> 00:04:42.215
Investigators brought with them a LumaLight,

00:04:42.582 --> 00:04:45.118
a device that makes blood and fingerprints visible.

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:50.890
The operator's goggles cut through the glare of the bright blue light,

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:54.193
so he can pick out clues that would otherwise go unnoticed.

00:04:56.029 --> 00:05:01.467
The light revealed a fine mist of blood droplets on Roxanne's ironing board and in her bedroom.

00:05:02.168 --> 00:05:02.668
See?

00:05:02.902 --> 00:05:03.503
Those smears?

00:05:03.569 --> 00:05:04.069
Yeah.

00:05:04.637 --> 00:05:05.305
Just like we thought.

00:05:05.305 --> 00:05:09.242
The search for a missing person suddenly became a murder investigation.

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:20.353
Anytime you have a surface with some blood on it and you hit it with a heavy force,

00:05:20.653 --> 00:05:22.889
it's going to fly out and spatter.

00:05:23.523 --> 00:05:24.757
And the harder that you hit it,

00:05:24.757 --> 00:05:25.858
the more force that you use,

00:05:26.259 --> 00:05:27.927
the finer that the spatter will be.

00:05:32.799 --> 00:05:34.701
Where was Roxanne Tandall's body?

00:05:39.138 --> 00:05:41.674
Prosecutors needed it to build a strong case.

00:05:43.343 --> 00:05:43.843
Without it,

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:45.745
her killer would likely go free.

00:05:49.415 --> 00:05:51.150
Detectives raced against time.

00:05:51.918 --> 00:05:54.020
In Hawaii's tropical heat and humidity,

00:05:54.454 --> 00:05:55.788
corpses decay rapidly.

00:05:56.956 --> 00:05:58.791
Telltale clues can be lost.

00:06:02.061 --> 00:06:06.265
Evidence tying Roxanne's death to her killer could disappear entirely.

00:06:13.773 --> 00:06:16.442
A major break in the case came on New Year's Eve,

00:06:16.476 --> 00:06:16.943
19...

00:06:16.943 --> 00:06:18.010
1989,

00:06:18.010 --> 00:06:20.279
two weeks after Roxanne's disappearance.

00:06:21.514 --> 00:06:21.981
Hey,
guys,

00:06:21.981 --> 00:06:23.683
I think I found something over here.

00:06:25.585 --> 00:06:27.787
Searchers found the body of a human female.

00:06:28.688 --> 00:06:30.289
She had been wrapped in two blankets,

00:06:30.757 --> 00:06:33.059
both tied securely by fabric strips.

00:06:35.395 --> 00:06:39.365
Marks on the body suggested she was strangled and hit forcefully on the head.

00:06:39.899 --> 00:06:42.268
We found our victim about a quarter of a mile south of our location.

00:06:42.335 --> 00:06:45.037
Although her face was blackened and her body swollen,

00:06:45.438 --> 00:06:48.474
family members recognized the remains as Roxanne's.

00:06:49.442 --> 00:06:50.643
It's going to be about 5.30.

00:06:51.377 --> 00:06:53.112
When investigators opened the blankets,

00:06:53.479 --> 00:06:54.580
flies emerged.

00:06:54.881 --> 00:06:57.984
Examiner and our forensics team to our location.

00:06:59.352 --> 00:06:59.685
All right,

00:06:59.685 --> 00:07:00.185
guys.

00:07:08.227 --> 00:07:08.895
The next morning,

00:07:09.195 --> 00:07:12.565
detectives called University of Hawaii entomologist Lee Goff,

00:07:14.901 --> 00:07:18.104
one of the most respected insect scientists in the United States.

00:07:25.545 --> 00:07:26.145
How are you,
Dr.

00:07:26.145 --> 00:07:26.979
Goff?

00:07:26.979 --> 00:07:27.780
Detective Glushenko.

00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:29.248
This is Detective Rufus Kalkani.

00:07:29.382 --> 00:07:30.049
Hi,
Doc.

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:31.250
Like it or not,

00:07:31.651 --> 00:07:34.120
insects are mankind's constant companions,

00:07:34.654 --> 00:07:38.291
thriving alongside us throughout our lives and well afterward.

00:07:40.493 --> 00:07:41.861
As a forensic consultant,

00:07:42.195 --> 00:07:45.198
Goff turns the abundance of insects to his advantage.

00:07:45.998 --> 00:07:50.369
His specialty is using bugs as a kind of biological stopwatch.

00:07:52.405 --> 00:07:56.742
If detectives hoped to tie this murder to Benjamin Tandall or to anyone else,

00:07:57.076 --> 00:07:59.412
they needed to get some answers from the bugs.

00:08:04.116 --> 00:08:05.885
Goff would act as their interpreter.

00:08:08.387 --> 00:08:09.589
Really,
when you're dealing with insects,

00:08:10.122 --> 00:08:15.595
you're looking at the largest group of animals that are on the face of the earth,

00:08:15.628 --> 00:08:16.896
the largest single group.

00:08:16.896 --> 00:08:18.264
A conservative estimate,

00:08:18.364 --> 00:08:19.165
very conservative,

00:08:19.699 --> 00:08:22.101
would give you three-quarters of a million described species,

00:08:22.201 --> 00:08:23.135
and in reality,

00:08:23.169 --> 00:08:24.937
I think it's probably much closer to a million.

00:08:28.074 --> 00:08:30.776
Insects compete with us for food and shelter.

00:08:31.577 --> 00:08:32.078
At death,

00:08:32.411 --> 00:08:35.181
we may actually become their food and shelter.

00:08:36.082 --> 00:08:37.583
That's what Gough counts on.

00:08:39.151 --> 00:08:40.620
When he arrived at the crime scene,

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:43.656
Gough was careful to collect all the insects he could.

00:08:45.191 --> 00:08:47.159
He snared flying bugs in a net.

00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:50.296
Others he picked off the body with tweezers.

00:08:51.797 --> 00:08:55.434
Gough would use the flies to pinpoint when the victim was killed.

00:08:57.470 --> 00:08:58.604
Because insects live,

00:08:58.971 --> 00:08:59.405
grow,

00:08:59.405 --> 00:09:01.274
and die in predictable intervals,

00:09:01.774 --> 00:09:04.076
their life cycles can be precisely timed out,

00:09:04.644 --> 00:09:06.178
sometimes down to the hour.

00:09:10.883 --> 00:09:14.253
In order to figure out the actual time of death,

00:09:14.253 --> 00:09:21.627
you have to have as complete a collection as possible of all of the insects that you find on the body.

00:09:23.162 --> 00:09:24.664
And for each of those insects,

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:32.338
then you have to know when they occur on the body and what the duration of their life cycle is.

00:09:32.905 --> 00:09:35.241
If you're working in the early stages of decomposition,

00:09:35.908 --> 00:09:37.209
say within the first two weeks,

00:09:37.877 --> 00:09:39.178
what you're really interested in are...

00:09:39.278 --> 00:09:41.514
are individual species of flies.

00:09:45.952 --> 00:09:47.486
In Hawaii's sultry climate,

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:49.622
insects thrived on the victims'

00:09:49.622 --> 00:09:50.222
remains,

00:09:50.623 --> 00:09:53.259
providing Gough with ample specimens to work with.

00:10:01.901 --> 00:10:05.237
You look for the most mature specimens that are there,

00:10:05.237 --> 00:10:08.040
but you try and get a general sampling of everything that's on the body,

00:10:08.841 --> 00:10:09.842
either on wrappings,

00:10:09.909 --> 00:10:10.409
clothing,

00:10:10.443 --> 00:10:11.644
or actually on the surface.

00:10:12.345 --> 00:10:12.712
Then,

00:10:12.712 --> 00:10:14.080
as the body is removed,

00:10:14.680 --> 00:10:16.682
you want to look at what's crawling around.

00:10:16.782 --> 00:10:18.050
on the soil underneath the body,

00:10:19.185 --> 00:10:19.852
very frequently,

00:10:19.919 --> 00:10:21.787
especially as decomposition progresses.

00:10:22.288 --> 00:10:29.462
You find that the center of arthropod activity shifts from the body itself to the kind of interface between the body and the soil.

00:10:29.562 --> 00:10:35.434
So you'll have a lot of things present there that are very significant in your estimation that aren't going to actually be on the body.

00:10:37.103 --> 00:10:40.206
Goff studied the samples at his lab at the University of Hawaii.

00:10:43.376 --> 00:10:46.379
After the female fly lays her eggs on a dead body,

00:10:47.046 --> 00:10:48.180
larvae soon develop.

00:10:49.015 --> 00:10:50.983
They're commonly called maggots.

00:10:59.058 --> 00:11:01.360
They secrete enzymes and spread bacteria,

00:11:01.761 --> 00:11:03.829
which enable them to consume human tissue.

00:11:06.699 --> 00:11:10.603
Gough believed that the maggots would tell the tale of the Tandall murder.

00:11:12.304 --> 00:11:16.342
But would they speak clearly enough to bring a murderer to justice?

00:11:21.313 --> 00:11:24.617
The life cycle of a fly is closely tied to our own.

00:11:25.518 --> 00:11:26.419
When a life ends,

00:11:26.686 --> 00:11:29.288
it's the beginning of a new generation of flies.

00:11:30.556 --> 00:11:34.560
A dead body is an invaluable source of food for hungry maggots.

00:11:35.995 --> 00:11:39.131
Flies begin to arrive moments after the host's death.

00:11:40.266 --> 00:11:45.071
Some unknown chemical from human remains attracts them from up to two miles away.

00:11:46.706 --> 00:11:47.773
As soon as they arrive,

00:11:48.074 --> 00:11:50.643
the females lay their eggs in protected areas,

00:11:51.343 --> 00:11:52.511
natural body openings,

00:11:52.812 --> 00:11:53.312
wounds,

00:11:53.479 --> 00:11:54.547
and folds of skin.

00:11:56.782 --> 00:11:57.683
Generally here in Hawaii,

00:11:57.783 --> 00:11:58.918
the species we deal with,

00:11:58.918 --> 00:12:03.255
you're looking at about 12 to 18 hours between the time the egg is laid and the egg hatches.

00:12:03.923 --> 00:12:04.757
Then you have your maggot.

00:12:05.124 --> 00:12:09.695
The maggots then congregate and they feed together,

00:12:09.729 --> 00:12:10.730
they form this mass,

00:12:11.230 --> 00:12:12.865
and they're going to move through the body together.

00:12:13.199 --> 00:12:14.433
So in any given mass,

00:12:14.533 --> 00:12:17.069
because not everybody lays their eggs at the same time,

00:12:17.603 --> 00:12:23.409
you may have maggots of several different species and quite frequently different age classes.

00:12:26.178 --> 00:12:26.946
As they mature,

00:12:27.413 --> 00:12:31.617
maggots pass through five stages that entomologists call instars.

00:12:33.886 --> 00:12:37.623
From infant to adult takes several days to several weeks,

00:12:37.957 --> 00:12:40.593
depending on species and environmental conditions.

00:12:42.428 --> 00:12:45.798
Figure out what species is invading a body at a crime scene,

00:12:46.599 --> 00:12:48.868
observe the particular stage of its development,

00:12:49.502 --> 00:12:54.707
and you can trace an insect's short life back to the time of death of its human host.

00:12:58.010 --> 00:13:02.414
Lee Goff did just that to determine precisely when the victim was murdered.

00:13:03.482 --> 00:13:04.683
Armed with that information,

00:13:05.050 --> 00:13:07.653
police could check the alibis of any suspects.

00:13:12.324 --> 00:13:15.761
The initial analysis showed about ten and a half days of insect activity.

00:13:16.362 --> 00:13:17.530
I knew it was longer than that.

00:13:18.030 --> 00:13:21.400
I knew there was a fair amount of literature to back that up.

00:13:23.769 --> 00:13:29.108
Goff suspected the tightly wrapped blankets may have delayed the fly's arrival by several days.

00:13:30.876 --> 00:13:37.483
He realized he would have to fashion an experiment to simulate the same conditions under which the victim's body was discovered.

00:13:38.951 --> 00:13:40.786
To determine when the victim was killed,

00:13:41.253 --> 00:13:44.023
Goff relied on a staple of biological research,

00:13:44.590 --> 00:13:45.491
a dead pig.

00:13:49.161 --> 00:13:50.329
But to try and pin it down,

00:13:50.362 --> 00:14:05.744
what I actually did was get a 50-pound pig and duplicate the wrappings and place the animal out in a somewhat similar situation and then determine how long it took for the insects to actually go down and get to the point where they could oviposit on

00:14:05.744 --> 00:14:06.244
the pig.

00:14:10.015 --> 00:14:14.153
Goff unwrapped the pig periodically to check for signs of fly eggs.

00:14:16.689 --> 00:14:20.593
Nearly all of the insects collected from the victim's body were either maggots,

00:14:21.060 --> 00:14:21.594
adolescent,

00:14:21.760 --> 00:14:22.895
or adult flies.

00:14:25.264 --> 00:14:26.332
Although decomposed,

00:14:26.599 --> 00:14:29.869
it wasn't out long enough to attract many other insect species.

00:14:33.772 --> 00:14:34.740
After the first couple of weeks,

00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:35.808
you start running out of flies.

00:14:36.909 --> 00:14:39.178
And then we have to go into what we call succession.

00:14:40.145 --> 00:14:45.084
And this works the idea that any insect that feeds on the body is going to change it.

00:14:46.218 --> 00:14:47.519
And by changing the body,

00:14:48.087 --> 00:14:50.356
then it makes it attractive to another group of insects.

00:14:50.990 --> 00:14:53.559
And their feeding makes it attractive to yet another group,

00:14:54.093 --> 00:14:55.261
and so on down the line.

00:14:58.097 --> 00:14:58.898
During succession,

00:14:59.365 --> 00:15:07.706
entomologists expect to see a more or less orderly transition from one insect species to another as the bugs arrive at a corpse and begin feeding.

00:15:09.942 --> 00:15:10.843
Depending on the weather,

00:15:11.277 --> 00:15:13.312
the numbers and types of insects vary.

00:15:16.248 --> 00:15:17.917
Gough altered the amount of sun,

00:15:18.417 --> 00:15:22.855
shade and water on the pig to mimic what may have happened to the victim's corpse.

00:15:28.727 --> 00:15:32.731
But how long did it take for the first egg-laying flies to arrive?

00:15:35.034 --> 00:15:37.436
Gough didn't need to wait long for an answer.

00:15:39.505 --> 00:15:44.743
He observed the first flies on the pig carcass two and a half days after it was left in the open,

00:15:45.210 --> 00:15:46.445
wrapped and bound.

00:15:49.148 --> 00:15:55.321
Gough added the time observed during the pig experiment to his initial time of death estimate of ten and a half days.

00:15:58.557 --> 00:16:04.964
He was convinced the victim was murdered no fewer than 13 days before her body was discovered on December

00:16:05.631 --> 00:16:06.131
31st.

00:16:07.566 --> 00:16:11.136
Those extra two and a half days made all the difference.

00:16:17.009 --> 00:16:25.284
Goff's estimate dovetailed almost exactly with the date of the victim's disappearance and the discovery of her body two weeks later on New Year's Eve.

00:16:33.325 --> 00:16:37.796
Neighbors told investigators they last saw her in the late afternoon of December

00:16:38.364 --> 00:16:38.864
17th.

00:16:40.332 --> 00:16:44.770
She was sitting stiffly in the passenger seat of Benjamin Tandall's pickup truck.

00:16:59.585 --> 00:17:01.220
Brought in by police for questioning,

00:17:01.687 --> 00:17:03.956
Benjamin agreed to take a lie detector test.

00:17:04.723 --> 00:17:05.424
He failed.

00:17:06.325 --> 00:17:07.960
Then he asked for an attorney.

00:17:16.468 --> 00:17:20.105
Officers charged Benjamin Tandall with the murder of his wife.

00:17:24.910 --> 00:17:26.178
As they prepared for trial,

00:17:26.512 --> 00:17:29.548
the prosecution's case was bolstered by additional evidence.

00:17:33.018 --> 00:17:40.225
Detectives discovered that one of the blankets used to wrap the victim's body matched a photo of a blanket on a sofa in her home.

00:17:44.530 --> 00:17:49.134
And the bloodstains found in her house during the police search matched her blood type.

00:17:55.040 --> 00:17:59.745
Benjamin Tandall was tried and convicted for the murder of Roxanne Tandall.

00:18:07.086 --> 00:18:07.719
On November 24th,

00:18:07.719 --> 00:18:09.588
1989,

00:18:09.888 --> 00:18:11.790
he was sentenced to life in prison.

00:18:15.627 --> 00:18:17.830
By establishing the timetable for the murder,

00:18:18.464 --> 00:18:21.767
Goff turned scattered clues into hard evidence.

00:18:23.102 --> 00:18:23.702
Whatever Dr.

00:18:23.769 --> 00:18:25.170
Goff did helped the case a lot,

00:18:25.471 --> 00:18:26.538
especially during trial.

00:18:27.106 --> 00:18:27.873
You have a victim,

00:18:27.906 --> 00:18:28.674
a female victim,

00:18:28.707 --> 00:18:29.875
who's missing for two weeks.

00:18:30.075 --> 00:18:34.780
Her husband is giving a story that she had left to go out to a party,

00:18:35.147 --> 00:18:35.614
to work,

00:18:35.614 --> 00:18:37.783
and then afterwards she might have gone out with some friends.

00:18:39.318 --> 00:18:43.589
You run into the possibility that maybe she was killed a week before the body was found,

00:18:43.622 --> 00:18:44.623
as opposed to two weeks.

00:18:45.424 --> 00:18:46.325
So I think during the trial,

00:18:46.391 --> 00:18:46.758
Dr.

00:18:46.758 --> 00:18:51.597
Goff's testimony supported a lot of the circumstantial evidence that we had against the defendant.

00:18:52.731 --> 00:18:54.566
as far as when she was killed.

00:18:57.870 --> 00:18:59.204
The bugs had spoken,

00:19:00.072 --> 00:19:05.911
and their wordless testimony allowed a committed team of investigators to bring a killer to justice.

00:19:08.013 --> 00:19:08.513
Elsewhere,

00:19:08.914 --> 00:19:11.416
thousands of miles away in the Tennessee mountains,

00:19:11.917 --> 00:19:14.219
insects helped identify a missing person.

00:19:19.825 --> 00:19:20.325
On a walk,

00:19:20.659 --> 00:19:22.995
stumbles upon bones that were picked clean.

00:19:27.332 --> 00:19:27.866
Nearby,

00:19:28.167 --> 00:19:34.439
a weathered skull held the clue that would help investigators make sense of the jumbled remains.

00:19:42.915 --> 00:19:43.849
Eastern Tennessee,

00:19:44.349 --> 00:19:46.618
nestled in the foothills of the Cumberland Mountains,

00:19:47.019 --> 00:19:50.055
is famous for its fresh air and small-town charm.

00:19:52.758 --> 00:19:57.863
But the rural location also makes it an ideal place to dispose of murder victims.

00:20:00.432 --> 00:20:02.734
In January 1989,

00:20:02.734 --> 00:20:06.905
police received a call that a man had discovered a leaf-covered human skeleton.

00:20:08.774 --> 00:20:12.044
Aside from some bits of tattered cloth and pieces of jewelry,

00:20:12.544 --> 00:20:15.347
little was left to indicate that this was once a living,

00:20:15.714 --> 00:20:16.648
breathing person.

00:20:19.351 --> 00:20:21.853
Police found no identification of any kind,

00:20:22.521 --> 00:20:24.890
no hints of who the victim might have been.

00:20:27.192 --> 00:20:28.727
They found no evidence of trauma,

00:20:29.328 --> 00:20:30.996
yet they suspected foul play.

00:20:33.298 --> 00:20:35.434
How else would the bones have gotten there?

00:20:38.937 --> 00:20:41.540
Detectives found little to tell them what happened,

00:20:44.810 --> 00:20:46.912
except for a dried out wasp's nest,

00:20:47.412 --> 00:20:49.047
long abandoned by its maker.

00:20:51.516 --> 00:20:52.884
Would it be enough of a clue?

00:20:56.555 --> 00:20:57.556
For help with the case,

00:20:57.990 --> 00:21:03.495
officers of the Tennessee Bureau of Investigation contacted forensic anthropologist William Bass.

00:21:05.564 --> 00:21:08.400
Bass is an expert at reading human remains.

00:21:10.335 --> 00:21:13.105
He first established the age and sex of the victim.

00:21:14.172 --> 00:21:16.141
From the shape and size of the bones,

00:21:16.441 --> 00:21:21.546
he concluded that the skeleton belonged to a female who was not yet 18 when she died.

00:21:22.648 --> 00:21:25.350
But the cause of her death remained a mystery.

00:21:26.685 --> 00:21:28.287
There were no gunshots in the skull.

00:21:28.320 --> 00:21:33.925
There were no damage to any of the long bones or the bones of the body.

00:21:36.228 --> 00:21:37.896
We did not recover the howeid bone,

00:21:37.896 --> 00:21:38.897
which is a bone in the neck,

00:21:39.531 --> 00:21:44.836
which is one that you get when you usually break if you strangle somebody.

00:21:47.806 --> 00:21:51.076
Although investigators were eager to find out how the girl died,

00:21:51.510 --> 00:21:56.415
they first tried to determine when she died in the hopes that it might lead to her identity.

00:21:57.382 --> 00:21:59.051
The boy lives right over here in this house.

00:21:59.051 --> 00:21:59.618
Where is he now?

00:22:00.519 --> 00:22:01.320
He went on home.

00:22:01.320 --> 00:22:01.920
In the summer,

00:22:03.155 --> 00:22:07.492
the soft tissue on the body in Tennessee will disappear very rapidly.

00:22:08.260 --> 00:22:12.497
You can go from what you and I are now to a complete skeleton in only about two weeks in

00:22:13.432 --> 00:22:14.833
July and August in Tennessee.

00:22:15.434 --> 00:22:16.268
If you die in the winter,

00:22:16.468 --> 00:22:17.202
it takes longer.

00:22:17.769 --> 00:22:19.204
But if you die in the winter,

00:22:21.606 --> 00:22:23.809
by the following spring or summer,

00:22:24.776 --> 00:22:27.879
the brain would have decayed and the cranial vault,

00:22:27.979 --> 00:22:29.981
which is the inside of the skull,

00:22:29.981 --> 00:22:32.651
the cranial vault would be dry by that time.

00:22:35.921 --> 00:22:36.888
At the crime scene,

00:22:37.289 --> 00:22:38.990
Bass looked at the wasp's nest.

00:22:40.058 --> 00:22:44.229
Knowing when it was made would help them pinpoint the victim's time of death.

00:22:47.566 --> 00:22:50.335
Wasps need a dry place to build their nests.

00:22:51.436 --> 00:22:58.877
Bass surmised the skull must have been on the ground for several months before it dried enough to become a suitable home for the insects.

00:23:01.546 --> 00:23:05.283
So what this tells me is in January when the skull was found,

00:23:06.752 --> 00:23:07.786
With a wasp nest in it,

00:23:08.019 --> 00:23:16.061
that individual had been dead and the skull dry the previous summer because this is when this wasp nest at least was built.

00:23:20.699 --> 00:23:21.967
To confirm his findings,

00:23:22.334 --> 00:23:27.205
Bass contacted forensic entomologist Neil Haskell to help him identify the victim.

00:23:28.340 --> 00:23:32.077
Haskell consults in criminal investigations all over the U.S.

00:23:33.779 --> 00:23:34.846
Like all insects,

00:23:35.180 --> 00:23:36.081
wasps live,

00:23:36.448 --> 00:23:36.948
breed,

00:23:36.982 --> 00:23:39.050
and die in predictable patterns.

00:23:40.919 --> 00:23:41.620
In Tennessee,

00:23:42.020 --> 00:23:44.623
they begin nest building in late April or May.

00:23:45.657 --> 00:23:46.158
By summer,

00:23:46.491 --> 00:23:48.126
the nest bustles with activity.

00:23:49.327 --> 00:23:49.827
Then,

00:23:49.861 --> 00:23:51.196
when the colder weather comes,

00:23:51.496 --> 00:23:52.764
the wasps die back.

00:23:53.732 --> 00:23:56.635
The cycle starts over again the following spring.

00:23:58.136 --> 00:24:06.578
Knowing this life cycle of this particular group of wasps enabled us to come up with a minimum time of the year that it took the wasps to build,

00:24:06.645 --> 00:24:10.982
plus another six to eight months for the skull to become cleaned and dry.

00:24:15.954 --> 00:24:18.757
In order for the queen wasp to have made her home there,

00:24:19.157 --> 00:24:23.261
the skull must have been completely dried out by spring 1988.

00:24:25.730 --> 00:24:27.232
Prior to the wasp's arrival,

00:24:27.532 --> 00:24:36.241
a brigade of egg-laying flies and carrion beetles must have foraged on the body and cleaned the skull late in the summer of 1987.

00:24:38.143 --> 00:24:44.249
Both Bass and Haskell concluded that the young girl died no later than mid-summer 1987.

00:24:47.018 --> 00:25:00.298
We coupled the developmental time of the wasp plus the normal insects that eat this carrion and came up with an estimate of at least a year and a half prior to finding this skeletal remains that the person probably died.

00:25:00.298 --> 00:25:01.233
Now that was a minimum time.

00:25:01.233 --> 00:25:02.300
It could have been a little longer.

00:25:02.868 --> 00:25:07.239
In reality it turned out that the body had actually been out there since

00:25:08.006 --> 00:25:11.643
February of two years prior to the time we found the remains.

00:25:14.145 --> 00:25:18.416
The wasp nest had given investigators the information they desperately sought.

00:25:20.519 --> 00:25:22.721
Once they knew how long the girl had been dead,

00:25:23.121 --> 00:25:25.156
they began their hunt for her identity.

00:25:26.725 --> 00:25:34.599
They narrowed their search to teenage girls reported missing in 1987.

00:25:34.599 --> 00:25:37.469
They also took note of the jewelry found near the body.

00:25:40.605 --> 00:25:44.309
Investigators pulled a report detailing the disappearance of a young girl.

00:25:47.646 --> 00:25:49.381
In a photograph attached to the report,

00:25:49.781 --> 00:25:52.817
she wore some of the same jewelry found at the crime scene.

00:25:57.088 --> 00:26:00.125
The girl's name was Michelle Denise Anderson.

00:26:01.760 --> 00:26:04.563
She was 15 years old when she disappeared.

00:26:06.097 --> 00:26:09.100
Denise was last seen at a party on January 9,

00:26:09.100 --> 00:26:10.435
1987.

00:26:11.570 --> 00:26:13.371
When she didn't come home the next day,

00:26:13.805 --> 00:26:15.273
her mother reported her missing.

00:26:17.442 --> 00:26:19.978
Until police knew more about her disappearance,

00:26:20.378 --> 00:26:22.280
they considered Denise a runaway.

00:26:26.384 --> 00:26:29.187
Investigators had solved only part of the mystery.

00:26:30.388 --> 00:26:32.691
They knew Denise's name and,

00:26:32.757 --> 00:26:35.327
because of her age and the condition of the bones,

00:26:35.627 --> 00:26:37.529
that she was healthy when she died.

00:26:39.531 --> 00:26:41.566
They are convinced Denise was murdered,

00:26:42.167 --> 00:26:43.735
but they still don't know how.

00:26:45.503 --> 00:26:48.139
And no one has ever been charged with her murder.

00:26:49.941 --> 00:26:51.242
The case remains open.

00:26:51.977 --> 00:26:53.878
The investigation continues.

00:27:01.653 --> 00:27:06.491
The stories that bugs tell can fill in the missing pieces of a death investigation.

00:27:08.393 --> 00:27:10.595
For detectives in an Indianapolis suburb,

00:27:11.129 --> 00:27:16.267
insects would provide the only leads to three bizarre and puzzling deaths.

00:27:26.144 --> 00:27:27.479
Counted in the trillions,

00:27:27.879 --> 00:27:31.316
insects vastly outnumber any other creatures on earth.

00:27:33.685 --> 00:27:38.990
Florida tropical biologist James Kastner has been studying bugs for 25 years,

00:27:39.457 --> 00:27:41.359
and the field is definitely growing.

00:27:44.796 --> 00:27:46.398
Well,
approximately 10 years ago,

00:27:46.598 --> 00:27:50.568
the estimate of total insect species in the world was maybe 2 to 3 million.

00:27:51.302 --> 00:27:51.970
More recently,

00:27:52.070 --> 00:27:55.874
there's been work done in the rainforest with fogging the treetops and the canopy,

00:27:56.474 --> 00:28:01.413
and this has now raised the estimate of total world insect species to 30 to 40 million.

00:28:06.117 --> 00:28:09.587
Insects usually go unnoticed or are shunned altogether.

00:28:10.522 --> 00:28:12.424
They're maligned for spreading infection,

00:28:12.724 --> 00:28:13.692
destroying crops,

00:28:13.992 --> 00:28:15.960
and otherwise making life miserable.

00:28:18.063 --> 00:28:20.799
But without carnivorous insects and their appetites,

00:28:21.366 --> 00:28:26.371
the carcasses of dead animals would remain where they fell until bacteria consumed them.

00:28:28.106 --> 00:28:30.208
Not everyone finds bugs distasteful.

00:28:31.176 --> 00:28:35.613
Studying insects and other so-called lower life forms is nothing new.

00:28:36.381 --> 00:28:40.218
It's just becoming more widely practiced and providing better information.

00:28:41.619 --> 00:28:46.224
Forensic entomologist Jason Bird believes insects have a lot to teach us.

00:28:48.359 --> 00:28:50.261
Forensic entomology has been around since

00:28:50.895 --> 00:28:51.663
13th century China.

00:28:52.464 --> 00:28:54.132
It's been used extensively in

00:28:54.566 --> 00:28:55.934
Europe and Australia,

00:28:56.634 --> 00:28:59.637
and it's apparently pretty slow to catch on in the United States.

00:28:59.971 --> 00:29:03.508
It's really only had its current widespread use since the

00:29:04.242 --> 00:29:07.178
1950s, and just within the past 10 years alone,

00:29:07.178 --> 00:29:09.881
it has enjoyed a resurgence of popularity.

00:29:12.016 --> 00:29:14.953
Despite its obvious benefit to criminal investigation,

00:29:15.520 --> 00:29:21.292
forensic entomology is not a field of study sought out as enthusiastically as medicine or law.

00:29:23.261 --> 00:29:25.764
The ranks of forensic entomologists are slim.

00:29:26.765 --> 00:29:27.298
At most,

00:29:27.565 --> 00:29:32.704
a few dozen insect specialists in the United States regularly assist with murder cases.

00:29:34.139 --> 00:29:36.274
People see bugs not as partners,

00:29:36.674 --> 00:29:37.575
but as pests.

00:29:39.878 --> 00:29:40.545
Unfortunately,

00:29:40.678 --> 00:29:46.284
most people treat insects as something creepy and something to recoil and be afraid from.

00:29:46.818 --> 00:29:51.723
And most of this is a cultural prejudice or just not understanding what insects are all about.

00:29:52.257 --> 00:29:56.227
I think anybody who takes the time to study insects and to learn how they live,

00:29:56.728 --> 00:29:59.697
learn the extremely interesting behaviors that they show,

00:30:00.265 --> 00:30:03.935
can't help but become interested in them if not enamored with them.

00:30:07.505 --> 00:30:12.644
The benefits can be many to those detectives willing to overcome their distaste for bugs.

00:30:14.012 --> 00:30:16.681
Insects can make or break an investigation.

00:30:18.016 --> 00:30:22.120
That's the message brought on this day by insect scientist Neil Haskell,

00:30:22.654 --> 00:30:23.822
who has come to Wilmington,

00:30:23.888 --> 00:30:24.689
North Carolina.

00:30:27.525 --> 00:30:28.793
His mission is simple,

00:30:29.527 --> 00:30:36.034
to introduce law enforcement to the exotic world of bugs and their ability to crack difficult cases.

00:30:37.569 --> 00:30:40.672
The ultimate goal is to acquaint the law enforcement folks,

00:30:40.939 --> 00:30:42.707
the crime scene investigators,

00:30:42.907 --> 00:30:44.042
and coroners,

00:30:44.042 --> 00:30:44.709
medical examiners,

00:30:44.742 --> 00:30:45.242
with the,

00:30:45.243 --> 00:30:45.610
first of all,

00:30:45.610 --> 00:30:49.647
the importance of using entomological evidence in death investigations.

00:30:49.681 --> 00:30:50.181
Second of all,

00:30:50.582 --> 00:30:53.818
to train those investigators how to collect,

00:30:54.652 --> 00:30:57.722
recognize and collect the proper specimens,

00:30:57.789 --> 00:31:07.098
and then how to preserve it and how to ship it and transfer it to a qualified forensic entomologist for evaluation.

00:31:07.398 --> 00:31:07.898
analysis.

00:31:10.535 --> 00:31:13.504
Although insects can't communicate in a conventional sense,

00:31:14.072 --> 00:31:18.776
their behavior on a dead body can speak volumes to those who know how to listen.

00:31:20.645 --> 00:31:29.587
Haskell uses dead pigs as stand-ins for dead humans when teaching police officers and evidence technicians the finer points of forensic entomology.

00:31:31.155 --> 00:31:32.257
In his training sessions,

00:31:32.657 --> 00:31:35.526
Haskell tries to simulate the real world as best he can.

00:31:35.526 --> 00:31:36.026
he can.

00:31:37.228 --> 00:31:40.231
He arranges the pigs as one might find a dead human,

00:31:41.065 --> 00:31:41.900
covered by brush,

00:31:42.533 --> 00:31:43.468
hidden out of sight.

00:31:44.202 --> 00:31:52.644
These pigs have been put out to attract the carrion insects that are forever present in most of our environments.

00:31:53.344 --> 00:32:01.052
And it has been successful because we do know that under these carcasses we're going to be finding some fly larvae.

00:32:03.121 --> 00:32:04.756
that have infested the carcasses.

00:32:05.590 --> 00:32:09.994
And this is what we would find on human remains that have been out for a period of time.

00:32:13.064 --> 00:32:15.633
Haskell's class is no place for the squeamish.

00:32:16.401 --> 00:32:22.640
But the things his pupils see here are no worse than what they're likely to find in a real murder investigation.

00:32:26.978 --> 00:32:28.613
It's time for their next lesson,

00:32:29.480 --> 00:32:30.648
collecting maggots.

00:32:32.450 --> 00:32:35.153
We're collecting some of the maggots as preserved specimens.

00:32:35.153 --> 00:32:37.956
We're also collecting some of the maggots as live specimens.

00:32:38.423 --> 00:32:42.193
We had the liver that we're going to be growing them on in what we call maggot motels.

00:32:42.860 --> 00:32:44.295
And today we're using liver,

00:32:44.829 --> 00:32:45.329
beef liver,

00:32:46.230 --> 00:32:47.298
and we put 15 or 20...

00:32:47.298 --> 00:32:50.435
20,
25 maggots in that and then close it up,

00:32:50.468 --> 00:32:53.137
place it in this can or container,

00:32:53.771 --> 00:32:55.073
and then allow them to grow,

00:32:55.206 --> 00:32:58.209
monitor them daily or every two days to see how they're doing.

00:32:58.676 --> 00:33:00.078
And then we can,

00:33:00.611 --> 00:33:05.917
eventually they'll grow to adults and then we can make positive identification of those specimens.

00:33:08.853 --> 00:33:12.323
Female flies are the insect's first strike on a corpse.

00:33:13.024 --> 00:33:14.058
If something dies,

00:33:14.425 --> 00:33:15.126
they'll find it.

00:33:16.694 --> 00:33:19.330
When a female arrives on decomposing remains,

00:33:19.731 --> 00:33:21.766
she goes into an egg-laying frenzy.

00:33:25.603 --> 00:33:33.644
The maggots are the result of the attraction of the chemical smells that are coming from the body that attract the adult flies in.

00:33:34.045 --> 00:33:42.220
Now the flies have developed and evolved over centuries to select out this specific,

00:33:42.387 --> 00:33:43.821
very specific kind of food,

00:33:43.988 --> 00:33:46.391
the rotting tissues of dead animals.

00:33:51.963 --> 00:33:54.232
Not all the fly offspring will survive,

00:33:54.999 --> 00:34:01.906
but the mother has given her ravenous young a fighting chance by choosing a ready source of sustenance.

00:34:05.176 --> 00:34:10.715
This female will deposit anywhere from 150 to maybe up to 400 eggs in a clutch,

00:34:12.283 --> 00:34:15.620
seeking out sites of protection such as nose,

00:34:16.020 --> 00:34:16.587
nasal area,

00:34:16.988 --> 00:34:17.488
mouth,

00:34:18.022 --> 00:34:18.656
and eyes.

00:34:19.424 --> 00:34:19.924
Those eggs...

00:34:19.924 --> 00:34:24.228
The eggs will then go through a period of time when they will eventually hatch,

00:34:24.629 --> 00:34:27.899
a few hours to a day or two depending on temperature.

00:34:29.067 --> 00:34:31.469
The eggs will hatch into first stage larvae,

00:34:32.070 --> 00:34:34.439
which will grow and develop for a few more hours,

00:34:34.739 --> 00:34:38.142
and then shed their skin and go through several more changes.

00:34:39.577 --> 00:34:40.611
As maggots grow,

00:34:41.212 --> 00:34:42.380
so do their appetites.

00:34:43.247 --> 00:34:44.749
During their first two stages,

00:34:45.049 --> 00:34:46.317
they never stop eating.

00:34:47.452 --> 00:34:52.090
The individual maggots form a large mass that eats and moves as one.

00:34:53.658 --> 00:34:57.195
And while they're in this maggot mass configuration,

00:34:57.962 --> 00:35:01.365
temperatures within this mass will climb very,

00:35:01.432 --> 00:35:02.100
very high,

00:35:03.167 --> 00:35:03.668
15,

00:35:04.202 --> 00:35:09.273
even up to 20 degrees centigrade above the normal outside air temperature or ambient temperature.

00:35:11.175 --> 00:35:11.943
Back in the lab,

00:35:12.310 --> 00:35:14.445
Haskell examines the mass of maggots.

00:35:15.413 --> 00:35:23.087
The particular stage and rate of growth of the larvae will provide investigators with a good idea of how long a body has been left in the open.

00:35:24.122 --> 00:35:25.890
The maggots act as filters.

00:35:26.791 --> 00:35:31.062
They can be tested for chemicals and drugs they may have picked up from their host.

00:35:32.396 --> 00:35:34.232
For those of us who know how to listen,

00:35:34.999 --> 00:35:39.504
insects can be vocal witnesses to the circumstances of a person's death.

00:35:43.674 --> 00:35:45.910
In November 1987,

00:35:45.910 --> 00:35:49.413
residents of a middle-class Indianapolis suburb phoned police.

00:35:50.681 --> 00:35:55.153
They were worried that they hadn't seen their elderly male neighbor for a number of days.

00:35:56.020 --> 00:35:58.856
He lived there with his invalid sister and aunt.

00:36:01.192 --> 00:36:02.460
Police arrived at the house.

00:36:03.261 --> 00:36:04.195
When no one answered,

00:36:04.529 --> 00:36:05.062
they entered.

00:36:07.932 --> 00:36:09.534
The house was a complete mess.

00:36:10.801 --> 00:36:13.704
Investigators found refuse of all sorts in the kitchen.

00:36:14.472 --> 00:36:15.373
Unwashed dishes,

00:36:15.840 --> 00:36:16.674
uneaten meals,

00:36:17.108 --> 00:36:18.209
discarded silverware,

00:36:18.476 --> 00:36:19.577
and empty packages.

00:36:24.815 --> 00:36:27.785
Bags of decaying food moldered in the garage.

00:36:33.758 --> 00:36:35.426
Then police found the homeowner,

00:36:35.826 --> 00:36:37.395
collapsed on the living room floor,

00:36:37.762 --> 00:36:39.697
apparently dead for some time.

00:36:43.568 --> 00:36:45.036
Not certain what they were facing,

00:36:45.503 --> 00:36:47.071
investigators called for backup.

00:36:50.174 --> 00:36:51.576
As more detectives arrived,

00:36:52.009 --> 00:36:53.511
neighbors began to congregate.

00:36:59.183 --> 00:37:02.386
Nearly every inch of the house was covered with trash.

00:37:08.159 --> 00:37:11.162
Police wondered what kind of person could live like this.

00:37:18.536 --> 00:37:21.372
The officers cautiously inspected every room.

00:37:24.542 --> 00:37:27.011
The search quickly took a bizarre twist.

00:37:30.181 --> 00:37:30.715
What in the ass?

00:37:30.881 --> 00:37:32.783
Gosh.

00:37:32.783 --> 00:37:33.350
Oh,
yeah?

00:37:35.753 --> 00:37:41.592
Investigators were shocked to find the mummified remains of two women in a pair of back bedrooms.

00:37:43.494 --> 00:37:46.030
Since the cause of death wasn't readily apparent,

00:37:46.497 --> 00:37:47.932
police assumed murder.

00:37:49.533 --> 00:37:52.670
Sergeant Reggie Roney recalls the grisly discovery.

00:37:56.173 --> 00:37:59.577
Treating this as a homicide scene because we don't know what has happened here.

00:38:00.411 --> 00:38:07.118
And my first view of the body is there's no body.

00:38:07.451 --> 00:38:13.157
All that's left is clothing and it's kinda like a mummy laying there on the bed.

00:38:13.924 --> 00:38:14.424
And

00:38:15.559 --> 00:38:23.301
I look at it and I can see the little skull caps that the older women used to wear and bed sheets.

00:38:24.001 --> 00:38:28.472
And I can tell all the bodily fluids have gone through the mattress,

00:38:29.307 --> 00:38:32.677
and it's just a skeletal-type mummy.

00:38:35.980 --> 00:38:39.050
The only evidence seemed to be an abundance of bugs,

00:38:39.750 --> 00:38:40.618
beetles mostly,

00:38:40.918 --> 00:38:42.620
found on the bodies of both women.

00:38:45.289 --> 00:38:48.626
Whatever happened here wasn't an ordinary death scene.

00:38:49.927 --> 00:38:53.297
It would take extraordinary means to sort it all out.

00:39:02.606 --> 00:39:06.711
Investigators on the scene of the triple deaths faced a double mystery.

00:39:07.712 --> 00:39:11.148
How did the victims die and how long had they been dead?

00:39:13.451 --> 00:39:15.853
Autopsies would probably answer the first question.

00:39:16.587 --> 00:39:18.923
But the second one seemed unsolvable.

00:39:20.991 --> 00:39:21.926
At the crime scene,

00:39:22.259 --> 00:39:25.429
detectives meticulously cataloged each piece of evidence,

00:39:25.930 --> 00:39:30.034
including several dead flies and the dried husks shed by beetles,

00:39:30.368 --> 00:39:31.769
called cast skins.

00:39:32.970 --> 00:39:35.172
Each insect type was a valuable clue,

00:39:35.973 --> 00:39:38.843
but investigators weren't certain how to read the evidence.

00:39:40.378 --> 00:39:46.083
They also gathered samples of an unusual brown stringy material found near both bodies.

00:39:47.852 --> 00:39:49.286
Baffled by what they found,

00:39:49.653 --> 00:39:53.090
they realized they needed the help of a forensic entomologist.

00:39:56.894 --> 00:39:58.763
They called on Neil Haskell.

00:40:00.865 --> 00:40:06.804
He met members of the Indiana University Medical Center forensic science team at the morgue for an autopsy.

00:40:16.747 --> 00:40:24.121
Obviously we had to determine how long these individuals had been there because in the investigation it was important to pinpoint time of death.

00:40:24.422 --> 00:40:26.290
Obviously who these individuals are,

00:40:27.458 --> 00:40:28.159
when they died,

00:40:28.359 --> 00:40:30.394
and possibly where they died and how they died.

00:40:30.761 --> 00:40:35.833
And so each one of the different forensic disciplines was providing pieces of that puzzle together.

00:40:41.705 --> 00:40:42.940
Right there.

00:40:43.007 --> 00:40:43.674
Okay.
For Haskell,

00:40:44.041 --> 00:40:49.447
that meant identifying the particular insect species that colonized each of the women's remains.

00:40:49.447 --> 00:40:50.247
Thank you.
And for...

00:40:55.286 --> 00:40:58.923
Haskell noted a curious difference between the two female mummies.

00:41:00.157 --> 00:41:02.927
One's face contained most of the original flesh,

00:41:03.427 --> 00:41:04.795
while the second did not.

00:41:07.064 --> 00:41:07.965
From the second body,

00:41:08.399 --> 00:41:10.868
Haskell collected numerous empty puparia,

00:41:11.168 --> 00:41:11.969
or casings.

00:41:13.103 --> 00:41:13.437
These,

00:41:13.437 --> 00:41:13.937
he knew,

00:41:14.004 --> 00:41:18.242
were left behind by juvenile blowflies as they hatched into adulthood.

00:41:20.377 --> 00:41:24.348
But the first set of mummified remains was marked only by dead beetles.

00:41:25.583 --> 00:41:26.650
Why the difference?

00:41:29.253 --> 00:41:34.658
Haskell knew that decomposing remains almost immediately attract egg-laying female flies.

00:41:36.527 --> 00:41:39.997
Judging from the amount of flesh still on the body of one of the mummies,

00:41:40.631 --> 00:41:42.766
flies seem to have ignored it altogether.

00:41:46.036 --> 00:41:47.738
Insects are weather-dependent.

00:41:48.506 --> 00:41:50.774
Their development is controlled by the seasons.

00:41:53.244 --> 00:41:56.180
Haskell suspected that weather had played a role.

00:41:58.916 --> 00:42:00.150
To gather more evidence,

00:42:00.417 --> 00:42:02.987
he visited the house where the bodies were discovered.

00:42:08.192 --> 00:42:09.159
In both bedrooms,

00:42:09.393 --> 00:42:11.395
he found beetle cast skins.

00:42:12.363 --> 00:42:14.932
But in one bedroom was an abundance of intact,

00:42:15.266 --> 00:42:16.066
dead beetles.

00:42:18.168 --> 00:42:18.802
In that room,

00:42:19.169 --> 00:42:22.072
he collected specimens of three different species.

00:42:25.109 --> 00:42:26.210
The blinds had been pulled down.

00:42:26.644 --> 00:42:27.144
They were down.

00:42:27.444 --> 00:42:28.412
But behind those blinds,

00:42:28.712 --> 00:42:30.281
as I raised the blinds and searched the room,

00:42:31.181 --> 00:42:41.258
there were a couple hundred adult dried beetles that had come from the body and had tried to reach the outer doors and gone to the windows and could get no further.

00:42:41.659 --> 00:42:43.160
And so then they died on the window sills.

00:42:46.196 --> 00:42:52.403
Haskell was still puzzled by the pencil shaving-like substance found next to the dead beetles on the woman's body.

00:42:54.538 --> 00:42:55.072
At first,

00:42:55.339 --> 00:42:57.274
he theorized it was a kind of fungus.

00:43:02.246 --> 00:43:06.250
Then he remembered that a similar material had been found on a body in Copenhagen,

00:43:06.350 --> 00:43:08.285
Denmark in 1962.

00:43:11.822 --> 00:43:12.690
In his laboratory,

00:43:13.157 --> 00:43:15.459
Haskell put the substance under a microscope.

00:43:17.227 --> 00:43:19.129
As others had in the Copenhagen case,

00:43:19.563 --> 00:43:23.667
he identified the substance as beetle feces excreted by the larvae.

00:43:25.235 --> 00:43:29.840
The feces is associated with remains that have been mummified for long periods.

00:43:37.247 --> 00:43:39.550
As detectives continued their search of the home,

00:43:39.883 --> 00:43:42.152
they found a diary written by the aunt.

00:43:46.290 --> 00:43:47.224
In her last entry,

00:43:47.658 --> 00:43:48.525
dated October 5,

00:43:48.525 --> 00:43:50.094
1977,

00:43:50.628 --> 00:43:52.630
the aunt reported that her health was failing,

00:43:53.130 --> 00:43:54.798
and so was the health of her niece.

00:43:56.667 --> 00:43:57.167
Neither,

00:43:57.334 --> 00:43:58.035
the aunt wrote,

00:43:58.435 --> 00:43:59.536
had long to live.

00:44:02.072 --> 00:44:04.808
This information helped Haskell with his investigation.

00:44:05.909 --> 00:44:07.344
He theorized that the ant,

00:44:07.678 --> 00:44:10.347
whose remains were more skeletonized than her nieces,

00:44:11.048 --> 00:44:13.517
must have died when the flies were still active.

00:44:14.551 --> 00:44:16.453
That would have been no later than autumn.

00:44:18.822 --> 00:44:23.360
The ant's body had less flesh because the flies arrived during warmer weather,

00:44:23.961 --> 00:44:24.728
laid their eggs,

00:44:25.095 --> 00:44:27.064
and their larvae had cleaned the skeleton.

00:44:29.533 --> 00:44:31.135
Haskell concluded that the aunt,

00:44:31.435 --> 00:44:32.469
the older of the pair,

00:44:32.903 --> 00:44:38.308
died first in October 1977.

00:44:38.308 --> 00:44:44.882
The niece likely would have died in late December 1977 or perhaps early January 1978.

00:44:46.950 --> 00:44:51.855
The niece's body was better preserved because her death came in the coldest part of the year.

00:44:52.956 --> 00:44:59.363
There simply were no egg-laying flies around to be attracted to the chemical signals emanating from her remains.

00:45:04.001 --> 00:45:06.937
The mystery of when the women died was solved.

00:45:08.105 --> 00:45:09.473
Other questions persisted.

00:45:10.474 --> 00:45:14.511
Why were the women kept in the house for ten years after they died?

00:45:15.612 --> 00:45:18.816
Police could only speculate on the details of their lives.

00:45:19.817 --> 00:45:22.820
The diary helped fill in some of the missing pieces.

00:45:24.288 --> 00:45:27.391
The aunt had fallen in the backyard and broken her hip.

00:45:28.092 --> 00:45:29.660
She could no longer get around.

00:45:32.062 --> 00:45:33.564
Soon after the aunt was injured,

00:45:33.797 --> 00:45:35.499
her niece also broke her hip.

00:45:36.867 --> 00:45:41.805
The man looked after both women around the clock as their health continued to fail.

00:45:43.307 --> 00:45:44.374
After his aunt died,

00:45:44.675 --> 00:45:48.979
the man decided to keep her in the house rather than alert anyone to her passing.

00:45:50.647 --> 00:45:51.381
Shortly after,

00:45:51.715 --> 00:45:52.716
his sister died.

00:45:53.584 --> 00:45:56.019
He decided to keep her death a secret as well.

00:46:08.932 --> 00:46:11.935
He managed to maintain the charade for 10 years.

00:46:14.538 --> 00:46:15.839
To avoid being discovered,

00:46:16.173 --> 00:46:18.942
he always ordered enough groceries for three people.

00:46:20.978 --> 00:46:26.650
He refused to allow the delivery person to bring them into the house for fear the smell would be noticed.

00:46:33.457 --> 00:46:36.593
He insisted that the bags be slipped under the garage door.

00:46:43.200 --> 00:46:47.304
That explained the sacks of uneaten food rotting in the garage.

00:46:59.016 --> 00:47:00.184
As the weather warmed,

00:47:00.484 --> 00:47:04.121
the odors from the bodies surely would have been difficult to live with.

00:47:09.459 --> 00:47:10.093
To the neighbors,

00:47:10.460 --> 00:47:11.662
the man was friendly enough,

00:47:11.995 --> 00:47:13.463
but he kept them at arm's length.

00:47:15.399 --> 00:47:17.801
When asked about the health of his aunt or sister,

00:47:18.135 --> 00:47:19.069
he'd be noncommittal.

00:47:20.070 --> 00:47:23.273
Sometimes he'd complain that the women were keeping him up all night,

00:47:23.874 --> 00:47:25.075
or give details of their...

00:47:25.209 --> 00:47:25.909
...failing health.

00:47:28.078 --> 00:47:28.579
Remarkably,

00:47:28.879 --> 00:47:32.549
the neighbors kept their distance and never set foot in his home.

00:47:34.818 --> 00:47:36.620
As detectives investigated further,

00:47:36.920 --> 00:47:39.523
the final piece of the puzzle fell into place.

00:47:40.490 --> 00:47:48.265
They discovered that the man received at least $140,000 in fraudulent Social Security checks after the women's deaths.

00:47:50.667 --> 00:47:52.636
But that may have only been part of the reason.

00:47:53.804 --> 00:47:58.842
Perhaps the decision to keep the bodies was motivated more by compassion than greed.

00:48:00.644 --> 00:48:06.483
He had cared for them so long and looked after them 24 hours on a daily basis.

00:48:08.185 --> 00:48:13.991
I think that he just probably couldn't bring himself to give up his loved ones.

00:48:17.394 --> 00:48:21.498
No charges were ever filed in the case of the Indianapolis Mummies.

00:48:25.269 --> 00:48:29.439
The trio's deaths were officially recorded as due to natural causes.

00:48:33.343 --> 00:48:34.311
Before they died,

00:48:34.645 --> 00:48:37.481
they named their church as the beneficiary of the estate.

00:48:39.950 --> 00:48:45.322
The Social Security money owed to the government was partially paid back by the sale of the property.

00:48:52.362 --> 00:48:57.067
The bugs had helped detectives solve the curious case of the Indianapolis mummies.

00:48:58.101 --> 00:48:59.102
However unusual,

00:48:59.503 --> 00:49:04.775
it was one investigation among hundreds for insect specialists like Neil Haskell.

00:49:06.009 --> 00:49:14.518
Haskell says that bugs are becoming powerful allies in an age when sophisticated technology is figuring more and more into criminal cases.

00:49:16.119 --> 00:49:22.459
One example is the analysis of human DNA found in the blood that bugs take from human bodies.

00:49:23.694 --> 00:49:26.229
The mosquito's feed and it swells up,

00:49:26.263 --> 00:49:27.264
its abdomen swells up,

00:49:27.431 --> 00:49:28.665
that's a human blood meal in there.

00:49:29.466 --> 00:49:34.004
And you can analyze that blood meal for human DNA.

00:49:35.339 --> 00:49:37.107
And it works in mosquitoes,

00:49:37.474 --> 00:49:37.974
bed bugs,

00:49:38.041 --> 00:49:38.541
fleas,

00:49:38.709 --> 00:49:39.576
and lice now.

00:49:40.911 --> 00:49:41.411
Tremendous,

00:49:42.512 --> 00:49:43.113
tremendous tool.

00:49:46.583 --> 00:49:51.355
The study of insects is the study of the great tenacity and variety of life.

00:49:55.459 --> 00:49:58.829
Yet modern science has much to learn about insect life,

00:49:59.363 --> 00:50:01.798
especially as it applies to investigating death.

00:50:03.700 --> 00:50:05.569
According to entomologist Lee Goff,

00:50:06.136 --> 00:50:08.238
many details are still unknown.

00:50:10.240 --> 00:50:14.578
We need to put forensic entomology on a firmer statistical basis.

00:50:15.445 --> 00:50:15.945
Right now,

00:50:16.046 --> 00:50:18.382
the statistical basis is just beginning to be developed,

00:50:18.448 --> 00:50:22.085
and we need to see more modeling in terms of what's going on with the corpses.

00:50:22.552 --> 00:50:23.920
So there's an awful lot of research.

00:50:23.987 --> 00:50:24.755
I think probably

00:50:25.489 --> 00:50:28.725
I've got more questions now than I had when I got into it 15 years ago.

00:50:31.528 --> 00:50:32.696
As methods improve,

00:50:33.130 --> 00:50:37.200
as more scientists enter the field and learn more about insect behavior,

00:50:37.634 --> 00:50:40.270
the value of forensic entomology will grow.

00:50:43.540 --> 00:50:44.441
Now when we first started,

00:50:44.541 --> 00:50:45.442
actually I go back to

00:50:46.076 --> 00:50:49.279
15 years ago when I first became involved in forensic entomology,

00:50:49.713 --> 00:50:52.382
law enforcement agencies thought we were a little bit nuts,

00:50:53.183 --> 00:50:57.354
and really were a little skeptical that we'd be able to give them anything that would be of use.

00:50:57.888 --> 00:50:58.455
over the past year.

00:50:58.455 --> 00:51:04.528
The past 15 years by working very diligently with different agencies and different groups of people,

00:51:04.594 --> 00:51:05.762
we've been able to convince them.

00:51:05.896 --> 00:51:06.296
In fact,

00:51:06.296 --> 00:51:10.467
it is a very powerful tool for use in their investigations.

00:51:10.500 --> 00:51:12.569
So I'd say now it's very widely accepted.

00:51:17.107 --> 00:51:19.643
No matter how technical our world becomes,

00:51:20.143 --> 00:51:22.879
the simplest methods are sometimes the best.

00:51:23.814 --> 00:51:28.051
And sometimes the least likely sources are the most dependable.

00:51:29.553 --> 00:51:30.053
Slowly,

00:51:30.587 --> 00:51:38.628
homicide investigators are beginning to realize the invaluable contributions made by the smallest witnesses to murder.

00:51:41.798 --> 00:51:45.068
A mother's search for her daughter spans two decades.

00:51:45.902 --> 00:51:48.538
She no longer knows what her child looks like.

00:51:50.040 --> 00:51:52.375
Will digital technology help locate her?

00:51:55.545 --> 00:52:00.183
A man slays his family and vanishes without a trace for 18 years.

00:52:02.285 --> 00:52:04.788
Can the work of a sculptor flush him out of hiding?

00:52:07.457 --> 00:52:10.260
Two hunters stumble across human remains.

00:52:11.928 --> 00:52:15.966
Can an artist put flesh on the bones and give the victim a name?

00:52:19.169 --> 00:52:21.171
Forensic artists combine science,

00:52:21.571 --> 00:52:22.072
intuition,

00:52:22.305 --> 00:52:26.143
and skill to build likenesses of the murdered and the missing.

00:52:29.312 --> 00:52:33.450
Their work can reunite families or help solve a murder.

00:53:10.253 --> 00:53:14.024
In the grey Texas sky in the winter of 1994,

00:53:14.457 --> 00:53:17.894
two hunters trespassed on private land outside of Lubbock.

00:53:19.362 --> 00:53:25.235
But their offense would pale in comparison to what they'd discover that day in remote Yellow House Canyon.

00:53:27.604 --> 00:53:30.640
They stumbled onto the partial remains of a human skeleton.

00:53:31.441 --> 00:53:34.511
and a white high-heeled shoe lying nearby.

00:53:36.947 --> 00:53:38.448
Though alarmed by their discovery,

00:53:38.949 --> 00:53:40.584
they knew if they called the police,

00:53:40.850 --> 00:53:42.319
they'd be fined for poaching.

00:53:46.723 --> 00:53:50.360
The hunters decided to leave the bones for someone else to find.

00:53:54.764 --> 00:53:56.866
But when they told a friend what they'd seen,

00:53:57.200 --> 00:53:58.301
he called the sheriff.

00:54:01.638 --> 00:54:07.344
The hunters agreed to take Deputy Tom Watson of the Lubbock County Sheriff's Department to the site of their find.

00:54:09.246 --> 00:54:13.083
Watson immediately suspected the remains belonged to a murder victim,

00:54:13.750 --> 00:54:15.952
dumped in the canyon to conceal the crime.

00:54:17.621 --> 00:54:20.323
Only the skull and a few bones remained.

00:54:21.691 --> 00:54:22.359
Over time,

00:54:22.792 --> 00:54:25.262
animals must have dragged away parts of the body.

00:54:26.596 --> 00:54:31.468
Watson directed his deputies to tape off a wide area to be searched for more evidence.

00:54:50.287 --> 00:54:52.722
Once the crime scene had been documented on film,

00:54:53.123 --> 00:54:56.359
he called in the county pathologist to examine the bones.

00:54:58.895 --> 00:55:02.299
The pathologist collected the bones and took them back to his lab,

00:55:02.565 --> 00:55:04.634
where they could be examined for signs of trauma.

00:55:05.268 --> 00:55:07.937
I don't believe we have any local people missing.

00:55:13.276 --> 00:55:15.412
He confirmed Watson's suspicions.

00:55:15.912 --> 00:55:17.380
The victim was murdered.

00:55:18.882 --> 00:55:21.951
The upper part of the skeleton bore multiple stab marks,

00:55:22.419 --> 00:55:25.021
enough to kill a person several times over.

00:55:27.824 --> 00:55:30.827
The case was now officially a homicide.

00:55:33.430 --> 00:55:37.400
Watson returned to the canyon the next day to sweep for more evidence.

00:55:38.868 --> 00:55:39.903
To capture the killer,

00:55:40.437 --> 00:55:42.305
he first had to name the victim.

00:55:43.740 --> 00:55:46.676
But his search uncovered no form of identification.

00:55:47.610 --> 00:55:49.913
The only clothing was the one shoe.

00:55:50.947 --> 00:55:52.115
To identify his victim,

00:55:52.449 --> 00:55:54.784
he desperately needed more to go on.

00:55:56.986 --> 00:56:00.523
The first thing we have to do is determine whether it's male,

00:56:00.623 --> 00:56:01.157
female,

00:56:01.191 --> 00:56:02.325
and approximate age.

00:56:02.892 --> 00:56:08.765
Check those statistics against any possible missing persons that we might have locally.

00:56:09.699 --> 00:56:11.167
If we don't find anything there,

00:56:11.234 --> 00:56:15.271
then we expand the search out statewide into New Mexico and Oklahoma.

00:56:16.039 --> 00:56:19.309
And then if we still haven't got her identified,

00:56:19.342 --> 00:56:20.744
then we go national with it.

00:56:21.945 --> 00:56:26.549
The search team recovered more bones and luckily almost all of the victim's teeth,

00:56:27.450 --> 00:56:28.518
but nothing more.

00:56:29.719 --> 00:56:35.492
No personal effects that could clue them in to who the victim was or when the killing occurred.

00:56:36.826 --> 00:56:42.432
Watson turned the bones over to a forensic pathologist who was able to narrow down the possibilities.

00:56:43.433 --> 00:56:45.969
He concluded that the victim was a white female,

00:56:46.436 --> 00:56:48.405
18 to 24 years old.

00:56:49.472 --> 00:56:53.610
The weathering on the bones suggested she'd been dead at least two years.

00:56:54.844 --> 00:57:00.316
Tooth fillings in the lower jaw and a crown in the upper jaw offered more identifying features.

00:57:01.418 --> 00:57:07.123
The teeth were x-rayed so they could be compared to dental charts of missing white females of the same age.

00:57:08.324 --> 00:57:09.292
Using the x-rays,

00:57:09.726 --> 00:57:11.995
Watson began a search to make a match.

00:57:13.096 --> 00:57:14.697
He chased dozens of leads,

00:57:15.165 --> 00:57:16.833
some from as far as New Jersey,

00:57:17.834 --> 00:57:22.272
but none matched the dental records of the woman found in Yellow House Canyon.

00:57:25.475 --> 00:57:27.944
His investigation was leading nowhere.

00:57:29.312 --> 00:57:29.846
Meanwhile,

00:57:30.113 --> 00:57:32.048
a killer remained free.

00:57:34.217 --> 00:57:38.221
Watson needed more than the victim's bones and teeth to identify her.

00:57:39.088 --> 00:57:40.790
He needed to give the woman a face.

00:57:41.825 --> 00:57:42.325
For that,

00:57:42.592 --> 00:57:46.529
he turned to one of America's leading experts on facial reconstruction.

00:57:50.600 --> 00:57:56.339
Karen Taylor has worked for the Texas Department of Public Safety in Austin for 16 years.

00:57:57.340 --> 00:57:58.374
As a forensic artist,

00:57:58.641 --> 00:58:03.646
she helps identify skeletons by recreating what the person looked like when alive.

00:58:05.315 --> 00:58:10.720
Her images are distributed by police and the media in the hopes that the remains will be identified.

00:58:11.955 --> 00:58:14.190
Using techniques she's developed over the years,

00:58:14.657 --> 00:58:16.659
Taylor has had remarkable success.

00:58:21.998 --> 00:58:26.903
She serves police jurisdictions throughout Texas and works on cases across the country.

00:58:28.037 --> 00:58:29.939
Her job keeps her extremely busy.

00:58:31.174 --> 00:58:38.081
The unidentified woman from Yellow House Canyon was one among many nameless persons awaiting a face.

00:58:42.485 --> 00:58:55.231
Deputy Tom Watson from the Lubbock County Sheriff's Office brought me the skull of an unidentified white female found in that area and he asked that I try to build a face on that skull to help him identify her.

00:58:55.965 --> 00:58:56.465
And

00:58:57.400 --> 00:59:04.407
The information was provided to me that had been developed by an anthropologist to examine the skeleton.

00:59:05.041 --> 00:59:11.948
And so I knew from Tom's reports that the person I was going to be drawing was a young white female.

00:59:12.882 --> 00:59:15.718
I unfortunately didn't have a lot of other information.

00:59:15.785 --> 00:59:16.619
There was no clothing,

00:59:16.686 --> 00:59:17.253
for instance.

00:59:17.320 --> 00:59:20.523
Clothing is a very good clue as to body weight for me,

00:59:21.324 --> 00:59:22.191
from skeletal remains.

00:59:22.492 --> 00:59:23.560
There was also no hair,

00:59:23.760 --> 00:59:26.462
which provides an added complication,

00:59:26.529 --> 00:59:28.197
especially in a case of a female.

00:59:28.798 --> 00:59:31.367
So because there was so little to work with,

00:59:31.367 --> 00:59:34.103
the case was a little more difficult than some of them are.

00:59:34.871 --> 00:59:37.640
Without the victim's clothing to use as a guideline,

00:59:38.141 --> 00:59:40.910
Taylor had to take her cues from the skull alone.

00:59:42.245 --> 00:59:44.147
When putting a face on a human skull,

00:59:44.647 --> 00:59:48.084
one of the most critical parts is determining how thick the flesh will be.

00:59:49.686 --> 00:59:56.793
Taylor relies on standard tables of measurements developed by forensic anthropologists and classified by sex and race.

00:59:59.028 --> 01:00:00.363
Based on these measurements,

01:00:00.630 --> 01:00:04.400
she cuts out rubber markers that match the average depth of the skin.

01:00:05.969 --> 01:00:10.239
She glues them onto the skull at 21 anthropological landmarks,

01:00:10.640 --> 01:00:12.108
like the cheeks and chin.

01:00:18.348 --> 01:00:19.649
When the markers are in place,

01:00:20.116 --> 01:00:21.718
photographs of the skull are taken.

01:00:22.819 --> 01:00:24.887
For Taylor to accurately draw the face,

01:00:25.254 --> 01:00:29.626
the photographs must be exactly life-size and not distorted at all.

01:00:31.327 --> 01:00:34.097
After placing transparent paper over the photograph,

01:00:34.530 --> 01:00:38.701
she uses the rubber markers as guides in drawing the facial contours.

01:00:40.336 --> 01:00:45.241
It's the first step in recreating the image that may lead to the identity of the victim.

01:00:48.011 --> 01:00:52.015
Taylor also relies on scientific formulas to draw in the eyes,

01:00:52.382 --> 01:00:52.882
nose,

01:00:52.949 --> 01:00:53.883
and other features.

01:00:55.351 --> 01:01:00.423
The average human eyeball is between 24 and 25 millimeters in diameter.

01:01:01.591 --> 01:01:03.493
Since this is also the size of a U.S.

01:01:03.559 --> 01:01:04.059
quarter,

01:01:04.594 --> 01:01:07.864
Taylor has a convenient tool for tracing in each eye.

01:01:09.399 --> 01:01:10.266
I also know from...

01:01:10.266 --> 01:01:19.208
from reading some ophthalmology texts that the average person has an iris diameter,

01:01:19.242 --> 01:01:20.643
an iris width really,

01:01:21.844 --> 01:01:22.712
that is between

01:01:23.513 --> 01:01:25.114
11 and 13 millimeters.

01:01:25.415 --> 01:01:30.953
And I have little red lines on this scale showing the average range.

01:01:33.690 --> 01:01:37.193
And I've checked it on a number of people and it seems to hold true for most everyone.

01:01:37.360 --> 01:01:40.063
Most everyone's iris will fall right in that range.

01:01:40.697 --> 01:01:44.400
Taylor relies on her knowledge of anatomy to draw in the eyelids.

01:01:45.601 --> 01:01:46.302
On many people,

01:01:46.602 --> 01:01:47.136
the orbit,

01:01:47.403 --> 01:01:48.204
or eye socket,

01:01:48.504 --> 01:01:51.140
has a raised piece of bone on its outer edge.

01:01:52.008 --> 01:01:54.944
The inner edge of the socket has another bony landmark.

01:01:56.913 --> 01:02:03.352
These are the points of attachment for the ligament that stretches across the eyeball and controls the movement of the eyelid.

01:02:07.657 --> 01:02:11.494
Our upper lids are curved slightly more than the lower lids.

01:02:13.096 --> 01:02:18.067
They tend to drop slightly over the top of the iris when looking at a person in life.

01:02:19.969 --> 01:02:22.171
Taylor next draws the victim's nose,

01:02:22.538 --> 01:02:26.175
relying on some rules of thumb and hints provided by the skull.

01:02:28.111 --> 01:02:28.778
Surprisingly,

01:02:29.345 --> 01:02:37.954
we do have some pretty good information about noses to help predict the look of a nose in life based on the bony clues.

01:02:38.588 --> 01:02:40.590
Because the victim was a white female,

01:02:41.023 --> 01:02:42.859
Taylor knows to add 5 millimeters.

01:02:43.159 --> 01:02:44.794
to each side of the nasal opening.

01:02:45.762 --> 01:02:47.764
That represents the width of the nose.

01:02:48.698 --> 01:02:50.333
If the victim were African-American,

01:02:50.733 --> 01:02:53.102
Taylor would add 8 millimeters to each side.

01:02:54.337 --> 01:02:58.541
A bony spine at the base suggests how far the nose projects.

01:02:59.776 --> 01:03:08.084
Because that little bit of bone tells me whether to tip the nose down or to tip it up or whether it's rather level.

01:03:10.086 --> 01:03:14.991
And so I'm looking at the profile to determine that angulation of the nasal spine,

01:03:15.391 --> 01:03:16.492
and that gives me a hint.

01:03:17.160 --> 01:03:17.927
In this case,

01:03:18.528 --> 01:03:20.997
this is quite an upturned nasal spine,

01:03:21.030 --> 01:03:22.932
so I want a tip of a nose that's up.

01:03:24.333 --> 01:03:25.301
She's a young girl.

01:03:25.868 --> 01:03:27.470
She's got a youthful little nose.

01:03:30.239 --> 01:03:33.509
And the nasal spine tells me it's turned up at the tip.

01:03:35.077 --> 01:03:36.345
When the mouth is relaxed,

01:03:36.612 --> 01:03:39.015
the lips tend to cover the six front teeth.

01:03:39.916 --> 01:03:40.650
When we smile,

01:03:40.950 --> 01:03:42.585
we flash as many as ten teeth.

01:03:43.653 --> 01:03:45.154
Taylor draws the unidentified...

01:03:45.254 --> 01:03:49.125
woman's mouth in a relaxed expression to give her a neutral appearance.

01:03:51.160 --> 01:03:54.564
So if I make a mark at the outside of those canines,

01:03:54.630 --> 01:03:57.433
that should give me an approximation of the width of the mouth.

01:03:59.168 --> 01:03:59.969
If you can see this...

01:04:00.169 --> 01:04:07.443
staining of the gum line at the edge of the enamel of the teeth gives us a ballpark idea of the vertical dimension for the lips.

01:04:08.644 --> 01:04:09.045
And again,

01:04:09.045 --> 01:04:10.379
I know that she's young.

01:04:10.379 --> 01:04:11.414
I know that she's white.

01:04:13.015 --> 01:04:15.785
So I'm thinking young white female as I draw this little mouth.

01:04:17.520 --> 01:04:18.754
From a faceless skull,

01:04:19.288 --> 01:04:21.991
Taylor will soon create the image of a woman.

01:04:23.593 --> 01:04:28.698
She hopes it will give a name to the victim and eventually named the killer.

01:04:32.368 --> 01:04:36.572
As Karen Taylor added the finishing touches to her portrait of the unidentified woman,

01:04:37.039 --> 01:04:38.875
the victim's face was reborn.

01:04:40.543 --> 01:04:42.945
The image was now ready for prime time.

01:04:44.747 --> 01:04:48.251
Taylor's drawing was shown on television and local newspapers.

01:04:48.718 --> 01:04:53.055
giving anyone who recognized the woman the opportunity to provide information about her.

01:04:54.290 --> 01:04:56.025
For Sheriff's Deputy Tom Watson,

01:04:56.425 --> 01:04:59.195
the broadcast was a defining moment in the case.

01:05:01.998 --> 01:05:08.504
This particular case was very frustrating for the first six months because we were not able to identify her.

01:05:09.438 --> 01:05:16.279
We were about to the end of all of our options as far as getting her identified until we did have the forensic.

01:05:16.445 --> 01:05:17.146
drawing made.

01:05:17.947 --> 01:05:20.349
And once we aired that on the media,

01:05:20.783 --> 01:05:25.721
within a week we had two callers gave us the name of the dead girl.

01:05:26.489 --> 01:05:29.892
The callers identified Taylor's drawing as Belinda K.

01:05:30.226 --> 01:05:30.726
Tillery,

01:05:31.227 --> 01:05:33.095
who'd been missing for two years.

01:05:34.330 --> 01:05:36.299
One of the callers was Tillery's mother,

01:05:36.699 --> 01:05:38.167
who had lost touch with her daughter.

01:05:39.135 --> 01:05:42.939
The face on the screen delivered an eerie shock of recognition.

01:05:44.240 --> 01:05:44.740
Now,

01:05:44.874 --> 01:05:46.609
with a name to go with the face,

01:05:47.109 --> 01:05:50.846
Taylor obtained a copy of Belinda Tillery's driver's license photograph.

01:05:51.914 --> 01:05:56.986
She compared it to her drawing to study the similarities that led to Tillery's identification.

01:05:58.087 --> 01:06:00.156
The resemblance was undeniable.

01:06:01.791 --> 01:06:07.496
It's narrow or rather close at eyes and yet very broad face,

01:06:07.697 --> 01:06:08.531
broad cheeks.

01:06:09.832 --> 01:06:16.072
And this small nose and the width of the nose proportionately is very close here.

01:06:17.840 --> 01:06:25.247
The mouth size is almost exactly right and the fact that there's a very wide chin favorably compares also.

01:06:29.952 --> 01:06:31.253
For closer identification,

01:06:31.620 --> 01:06:36.158
Taylor superimposed the photograph of Belinda Tillery onto the victim's skull.

01:06:38.194 --> 01:06:43.332
As I was able to get the facial image on top of the skull and start to compare it,

01:06:43.366 --> 01:06:45.668
I had a couple of difficulties by this method,

01:06:46.435 --> 01:06:49.939
one being that her face is slightly turned in her driver's license photo,

01:06:50.006 --> 01:06:53.042
therefore I can't quite get the nose position correct.

01:06:53.709 --> 01:06:54.209
However,

01:06:54.243 --> 01:06:56.946
when I put the eyes pretty much centered in the orbits,

01:06:57.046 --> 01:07:04.253
I see that she does have the facial width that my drawing shows and that I can see in the skull.

01:07:04.787 --> 01:07:05.287
So,

01:07:05.955 --> 01:07:07.823
I can see that it's a pretty favorable...

01:07:08.057 --> 01:07:27.777
comparison and we probably should go ahead with finding dental records on this person if possible to make a more positive comparison a positive identification couldn't be made until x-rays of the victim's teeth were compared with dental records of belinda tillery dr

01:07:27.843 --> 01:07:33.516
david dual a forensic dentist with the texas department of public safety made the comparison

01:07:35.151 --> 01:07:37.386
He found the bone structure to be the same.

01:07:38.754 --> 01:07:40.389
Teeth with fillings matched.

01:07:41.857 --> 01:07:44.693
Both x-rays showed a root canal on the same tooth,

01:07:45.561 --> 01:07:48.431
and a temporary crown was identical on each film.

01:07:50.166 --> 01:07:50.533
Dr.

01:07:50.533 --> 01:07:52.234
Duell declared it a match.

01:07:53.135 --> 01:07:55.371
Taylor's work had identified the victim.

01:07:58.274 --> 01:08:01.977
It was now up to Deputy Watson to track down the killer.

01:08:04.413 --> 01:08:08.084
He started by interviewing Belinda Tillery's friends and neighbors.

01:08:10.052 --> 01:08:14.657
Watson learned that Tillery had had an abusive boyfriend named Troy Armstrong.

01:08:15.858 --> 01:08:20.663
It was rumored that he'd become angry when she told him she was pregnant with someone else's child.

01:08:23.999 --> 01:08:27.336
Watson obtained a photograph of Armstrong and went looking for him.

01:08:28.404 --> 01:08:29.772
He tracked him to Roswell,

01:08:29.839 --> 01:08:30.406
New Mexico.

01:08:31.640 --> 01:08:32.041
There,

01:08:32.041 --> 01:08:33.075
he searched the town.

01:08:33.943 --> 01:08:35.177
He questioned the locals,

01:08:35.544 --> 01:08:37.546
digging for any lead he could find.

01:08:38.514 --> 01:08:39.849
But Armstrong was gone.

01:08:41.250 --> 01:08:44.053
He'd found out that he was a suspect in Belinda's murder.

01:08:48.891 --> 01:08:49.625
Back in Lubbock,

01:08:50.025 --> 01:08:51.994
as Watson gathered more information,

01:08:52.394 --> 01:08:55.531
a picture of Belinda's last days became clearer.

01:09:00.102 --> 01:09:02.404
It had been two years since anyone had seen her.

01:09:03.405 --> 01:09:06.675
That's when some folks heard she'd gone camping with Armstrong.

01:09:08.310 --> 01:09:10.379
He liked to camp in Yellow House Canyon.

01:09:11.113 --> 01:09:13.282
It was a remote place that he knew well.

01:09:19.054 --> 01:09:19.989
What's going on front,

01:09:19.989 --> 01:09:20.422
all right?

01:09:20.422 --> 01:09:21.056
I'll catch up with you,

01:09:21.056 --> 01:09:21.457
okay?

01:09:21.457 --> 01:09:21.790
All right,
well,

01:09:21.790 --> 01:09:26.028
come on.
People mentioned that he carried a large hunting knife almost everywhere he went.

01:09:28.063 --> 01:09:29.765
The pieces were fitting together.

01:09:30.699 --> 01:09:36.906
It seemed that a bitter Armstrong had planned to take Belinda to this remote canyon and get rid of her for good.

01:09:38.174 --> 01:09:40.576
He thought no one would find her remains.

01:09:41.877 --> 01:09:43.012
After Belinda's death,

01:09:43.245 --> 01:09:47.149
he told a girlfriend he had nightmares about stabbing someone in the back.

01:09:49.685 --> 01:09:50.719
Based on the evidence,

01:09:51.020 --> 01:09:54.156
Watson obtained a warrant for Troy Armstrong's arrest.

01:09:54.957 --> 01:09:57.826
But he still had no information on his whereabouts.

01:09:59.929 --> 01:10:00.930
More than a year later,

01:10:01.263 --> 01:10:03.132
in January 1996,

01:10:03.599 --> 01:10:04.767
Watson received a tip.

01:10:06.001 --> 01:10:11.907
He found out that Armstrong was on the road with a truck driver buddy who was hauling for a company in California.

01:10:13.876 --> 01:10:16.212
Watson quickly contacted the trucking company.

01:10:17.680 --> 01:10:22.818
He discovered that their trucks were equipped with a global positioning system called GPS.

01:10:24.486 --> 01:10:27.990
A transponder on each truck bounced a signal off a satellite,

01:10:28.390 --> 01:10:30.893
telling the company where it was at all times.

01:10:31.994 --> 01:10:36.065
They informed him that the truck he was looking for was now traveling through Nebraska.

01:10:37.233 --> 01:10:37.833
Within minutes,

01:10:38.234 --> 01:10:43.138
Watson got the Nebraska State Police on the other line and asked them to set up a roadblock.

01:10:44.206 --> 01:10:46.842
They dispatched several cars to intercept the truck.

01:10:47.876 --> 01:10:48.844
As the police waited,

01:10:49.178 --> 01:10:52.248
they maintained contact with Watson and the trucking company,

01:10:52.648 --> 01:10:55.985
keeping tabs on the truck's whereabouts at all times.

01:10:57.386 --> 01:10:59.455
They knew just when to expect it.

01:11:09.498 --> 01:11:11.734
When the truck came barreling down the highway,

01:11:12.101 --> 01:11:14.136
the driver saw the roadblock ahead.

01:11:15.037 --> 01:11:16.038
He tried to escape,

01:11:16.438 --> 01:11:18.107
but the police sprang into action.

01:11:20.309 --> 01:11:21.343
Once the truck was halted,

01:11:21.610 --> 01:11:22.678
the police moved in.

01:11:24.847 --> 01:11:27.383
Hiding inside was Troy Armstrong.

01:11:29.118 --> 01:11:31.920
He was arrested for the murder of Belinda Tillery.

01:11:33.322 --> 01:11:33.822
By now,

01:11:34.123 --> 01:11:35.524
evidence against him had mounted.

01:11:36.225 --> 01:11:40.229
A former girlfriend and others admitted that he had confessed to killing Tillery.

01:11:42.898 --> 01:11:43.332
December 5th,

01:11:43.332 --> 01:11:45.034
1996,

01:11:45.501 --> 01:11:49.371
Armstrong was convicted for murder and sentenced to life in prison.

01:11:51.473 --> 01:11:54.543
Karen Taylor's lifelike reconstruction of Belinda K.

01:11:54.710 --> 01:11:58.047
Tillery was the key to solving a difficult case.

01:11:58.947 --> 01:11:59.447
Without it,

01:11:59.748 --> 01:12:01.884
the victim would never have been identified,

01:12:02.284 --> 01:12:04.987
and the killer never brought to justice.

01:12:07.589 --> 01:12:09.491
Like the artist she was trained to be,

01:12:10.225 --> 01:12:13.529
Taylor relies on pen and paper to capture likenesses.

01:12:14.663 --> 01:12:15.163
Elsewhere,

01:12:15.497 --> 01:12:19.868
scientists are beginning to apply digital technology to achieve a similar goal.

01:12:20.969 --> 01:12:23.472
At Colorado State University in Fort Collins,

01:12:23.839 --> 01:12:28.677
they're developing equipment to match faces to skulls with laser beam accuracy.

01:12:29.978 --> 01:12:37.486
Mechanical engineering professor Patrick Fitzhorn and a graduate student combined computer-aided design with laser technology.

01:12:38.520 --> 01:12:43.525
They created a system that scans three-dimensional objects and reconstructs them in a computer.

01:12:44.426 --> 01:12:48.230
So what this machine can do is build a three-dimensional surface mesh,

01:12:48.364 --> 01:12:49.365
computer surface mesh,

01:12:49.965 --> 01:12:54.002
of a human skull that we can then play with on the computer.

01:12:54.269 --> 01:12:55.871
We can scale it up and down.

01:12:55.871 --> 01:12:57.306
We can move it through space.

01:12:58.941 --> 01:13:01.377
As the skull rotates slowly on the platter,

01:13:01.777 --> 01:13:05.647
a laser beam strikes it at 10,000 to 50,000 points.

01:13:06.582 --> 01:13:10.953
A sensor measures the distance the beam travels as it reflects off each point.

01:13:12.054 --> 01:13:13.689
The results are fed into a computer

01:13:13.956 --> 01:13:16.091
that creates a digital version of the skull.

01:13:16.992 --> 01:13:18.260
The image is virtual,

01:13:18.794 --> 01:13:20.696
but its application is real.

01:13:21.864 --> 01:13:31.373
And obviously what we want to do with it then at that point is to fix it in space to take a scanned facial photograph and to map the photograph on the skull to see whether there's a match or not.

01:13:32.674 --> 01:13:36.311
A photograph of the would-be owner of the skull is also scanned.

01:13:37.079 --> 01:13:39.748
The digital photo can be cleaned up and sharpened.

01:13:40.883 --> 01:13:41.383
Here,

01:13:41.450 --> 01:13:43.919
Fitzhorn made the photograph semi-transparent,

01:13:44.253 --> 01:13:46.321
so he could superimpose it over the skull.

01:13:47.356 --> 01:13:49.258
Now he can compare their features.

01:13:50.325 --> 01:13:52.261
Here you can see the teeth in here.

01:13:52.961 --> 01:13:54.263
There's the bridge of the nose.

01:13:54.329 --> 01:13:55.264
Here are the eyes.

01:13:56.365 --> 01:13:58.233
There's the eyeball and its orb.

01:13:59.001 --> 01:14:00.536
There's the other eyeball and its orb.

01:14:01.270 --> 01:14:05.040
There's the end of the cheekbone here.

01:14:06.341 --> 01:14:08.477
The jawline down in here.

01:14:10.412 --> 01:14:10.912
Currently,

01:14:11.113 --> 01:14:16.385
a forensic artist relies on his best guess to determine whether a photograph matches a skull.

01:14:17.319 --> 01:14:20.789
He takes measurements and superimposes a photograph over a skull.

01:14:21.790 --> 01:14:23.959
But if the picture was taken from an odd angle,

01:14:24.226 --> 01:14:25.828
it could distort the results.

01:14:28.630 --> 01:14:31.433
And now what we're seeing is how the skull is oriented.

01:14:31.533 --> 01:14:35.270
You can see how it's rotated in here with respect to the photograph.

01:14:35.304 --> 01:14:35.804
So here,

01:14:36.638 --> 01:14:39.241
this is the plane that the photograph is in,

01:14:39.241 --> 01:14:42.811
and you can see the skewing of the skull with respect to it.

01:14:42.811 --> 01:14:43.311
Again,

01:14:43.545 --> 01:14:45.047
trying to line the skull up.

01:14:45.447 --> 01:14:52.421
in orientation with respect to the way the face was oriented when the camera photograph was taken.

01:14:53.722 --> 01:14:59.761
Fitzhorn can rotate the skull and reorient the scanned facial photograph to minimize the distortion.

01:15:01.129 --> 01:15:04.933
Some photos aren't clear enough or are too distorted to enhance,

01:15:05.334 --> 01:15:08.470
defying efforts to make a match that's 100%

01:15:08.604 --> 01:15:09.104
certain.

01:15:09.671 --> 01:15:13.275
But Fitzhorn is convinced that his method is much more precise,

01:15:13.742 --> 01:15:15.844
less subjective than previous methods.

01:15:17.079 --> 01:15:24.019
One of the things that I'm really excited about is the potential to take four or five points on the skull,

01:15:24.453 --> 01:15:27.256
to take four or five matching points on the face,

01:15:27.689 --> 01:15:31.460
and actually attempt to compute whether or not there's a match.

01:15:32.060 --> 01:15:39.835
What we'd like to be able to do is numerically compute this fit on the computer and then be able to say something like,

01:15:40.469 --> 01:15:41.970
this is a 95%

01:15:42.337 --> 01:15:45.240
probability of match or this is a 35%

01:15:45.574 --> 01:15:46.842
probability of match.

01:15:47.943 --> 01:15:52.214
As Patrick Fitzhorn and his colleagues perfect their computer face matching system,

01:15:52.781 --> 01:15:55.751
forensic artists in Louisiana are on a different mission.

01:15:57.052 --> 01:16:00.422
Instead of applying digital technology to identify the dead,

01:16:01.156 --> 01:16:03.492
they're hoping to use it to find the living.

01:16:04.293 --> 01:16:04.927
In this case,

01:16:05.193 --> 01:16:08.130
a girl who's been missing for over 20 years.

01:16:12.534 --> 01:16:13.835
In 1976,

01:16:14.202 --> 01:16:15.871
in a small town in Louisiana,

01:16:16.271 --> 01:16:18.173
a family argument turned sour.

01:16:19.775 --> 01:16:23.645
It wasn't the first time Geneva Martin and her teenage daughter argued.

01:16:24.546 --> 01:16:28.784
But what started out as a typical mother-daughter conflict soon turned into a

01:16:29.484 --> 01:16:31.587
21-year ordeal for Geneva Martin.

01:16:35.490 --> 01:16:36.858
She was 16 years old,

01:16:36.858 --> 01:16:37.358
you know.

01:16:39.795 --> 01:16:41.196
She let her opinion be known,

01:16:41.296 --> 01:16:43.065
and I let my opinion be known.

01:16:47.169 --> 01:16:49.071
She wasn't any more wrong than I was.

01:16:49.871 --> 01:16:50.772
The argument ended,

01:16:51.073 --> 01:16:52.007
as many before.

01:16:52.908 --> 01:16:56.078
The two went back to their chores without saying a word to each other.

01:16:57.312 --> 01:16:59.114
Then Donna decided to leave.

01:17:02.451 --> 01:17:04.586
She said she was going to her grandmother's house.

01:17:05.287 --> 01:17:06.755
It was 2.30 p.m.

01:17:10.392 --> 01:17:11.026
By 5,

01:17:11.093 --> 01:17:12.194
she hadn't returned,

01:17:12.561 --> 01:17:15.597
so her concerned mother went looking for her at her grandmother's.

01:17:16.298 --> 01:17:17.466
But Donna wasn't there.

01:17:18.767 --> 01:17:22.804
Her grandmother said Donna had gone next door to play cards with her Aunt Peggy.

01:17:23.939 --> 01:17:24.306
Well,

01:17:24.306 --> 01:17:25.507
didn't think anything about it,

01:17:25.507 --> 01:17:26.108
went back home.

01:17:28.110 --> 01:17:29.778
When she wasn't home at 9 o'clock,

01:17:30.545 --> 01:17:31.045
we went to

01:17:31.380 --> 01:17:34.383
Peggy's, and there was no Donna.

01:17:35.984 --> 01:17:38.420
Donna Martin had run away from home before,

01:17:39.287 --> 01:17:42.457
but this time it seemed like she wasn't coming back.

01:17:43.458 --> 01:17:45.260
Despite many attempts to find her,

01:17:45.794 --> 01:17:49.831
almost a year passed before Geneva Martin heard any news of her daughter.

01:17:52.701 --> 01:17:53.201
Then,

01:17:53.402 --> 01:18:00.642
one of Donna's friends told Geneva she had picked up Donna on the road and given her a ride to an apartment she was sharing in Baton Rouge.

01:18:02.044 --> 01:18:02.544
Geneva,

01:18:03.011 --> 01:18:05.414
desperately wanting to patch things up with her daughter,

01:18:05.781 --> 01:18:06.682
went to find her.

01:18:07.849 --> 01:18:08.817
But when she got there,

01:18:09.117 --> 01:18:10.419
the apartment was vacant.

01:18:11.553 --> 01:18:14.790
All that remained was Donna's name on the mailbox.

01:18:16.024 --> 01:18:18.827
The manager confirmed that Donna had been living there.

01:18:20.128 --> 01:18:21.730
I took a picture of Donna to her,

01:18:21.863 --> 01:18:22.531
and she told me,

01:18:22.564 --> 01:18:22.964
yes,

01:18:22.964 --> 01:18:27.803
this was the young girl that had lived in the apartment with another married couple.

01:18:30.572 --> 01:18:31.440
From there,

01:18:31.573 --> 01:18:32.441
we found nothing.

01:18:34.109 --> 01:18:36.578
Despite all she did to find her daughter,

01:18:36.978 --> 01:18:40.115
the trail remained cold for more than 20 years.

01:18:41.016 --> 01:18:41.516
Today,

01:18:41.850 --> 01:18:47.055
Geneva Martin hopes researchers at Louisiana State University will help heat it up again.

01:18:48.690 --> 01:18:56.431
Forensic anthropologist Mary Mannheim and forensic artist Eileen Barrow use computers to simulate the aging process.

01:18:57.399 --> 01:19:02.003
If they can produce an accurate likeness of Donna Martin today at age 37,

01:19:02.637 --> 01:19:05.240
her mother may have a chance of finding her.

01:19:07.743 --> 01:19:11.546
LSU's Forensic Anthropology and Computer Enhancement Services,

01:19:11.980 --> 01:19:12.781
or FACES,

01:19:13.281 --> 01:19:17.619
is an ambitious program that's collecting data on the subtleties of the human face.

01:19:19.087 --> 01:19:25.227
among its objectives to develop precise standards of measurement for tissue thickness on the human skull.

01:19:26.328 --> 01:19:31.533
The information is used by forensic artists trying to build faces from bare bones.

01:19:32.501 --> 01:19:33.101
Until now,

01:19:33.468 --> 01:19:35.937
they relied on measurements based on cadavers.

01:19:37.672 --> 01:19:42.377
Mannheim believes that using live subjects will produce much more accurate measurements.

01:19:44.379 --> 01:19:45.881
By using living models,

01:19:46.281 --> 01:19:50.619
Mannheim can select from a much broader and more complete statistical range.

01:19:51.820 --> 01:19:53.288
Though subjects remain anonymous,

01:19:53.688 --> 01:19:54.322
their age,

01:19:54.623 --> 01:19:55.123
sex,

01:19:55.423 --> 01:19:55.923
race,

01:19:56.124 --> 01:19:56.624
height,

01:19:56.858 --> 01:19:57.358
weight,

01:19:57.392 --> 01:20:01.029
and other personal information becomes part of the computer's database.

01:20:02.164 --> 01:20:03.532
To measure tissue thickness,

01:20:03.932 --> 01:20:05.767
she uses an ultrasound scanner,

01:20:06.401 --> 01:20:08.737
like the ones used to show a fetus in the womb.

01:20:09.905 --> 01:20:12.374
The scanner bounces sound waves off the skull.

01:20:13.508 --> 01:20:16.244
By measuring the time it takes for waves to return,

01:20:16.678 --> 01:20:21.183
a computer calculates the exact depth of the tissue from skin to bone.

01:20:22.317 --> 01:20:25.821
The process is repeated at 19 points across the skull.

01:20:25.854 --> 01:20:28.824
We've scanned over

01:20:29.391 --> 01:20:31.359
400, almost 500 people.

01:20:32.093 --> 01:20:36.832
And those range in age from two years of age to approximately 74 years of age.

01:20:37.199 --> 01:20:38.500
There are many different sizes,

01:20:38.667 --> 01:20:39.935
many different racial groups,

01:20:40.202 --> 01:20:42.103
so we will separate these out,

01:20:42.270 --> 01:20:42.770
height,

01:20:42.771 --> 01:20:43.271
weight,

01:20:43.271 --> 01:20:43.572
age,

01:20:43.572 --> 01:20:44.072
etc.,

01:20:44.472 --> 01:20:46.041
and we will produce standards.

01:20:46.875 --> 01:20:50.712
Mannheim has scanned the faces of generations of the same family.

01:20:51.613 --> 01:20:55.450
The data will show how family resemblances change over time.

01:20:56.718 --> 01:21:01.256
Mannheim and her colleagues are also using computers to show what a missing child's face

01:21:01.456 --> 01:21:03.491
might look like as it grows older.

01:21:04.659 --> 01:21:05.560
With young children,

01:21:05.894 --> 01:21:10.065
the first step is stretching the child's features to reflect growth of the skull.

01:21:11.099 --> 01:21:11.766
In this case,

01:21:12.067 --> 01:21:16.171
a five-year-old child disappeared and has been missing five years.

01:21:17.005 --> 01:21:19.307
Barrow renders the child at age 10.

01:21:20.508 --> 01:21:23.945
The computer's stretching tool divides the face into a grid.

01:21:24.980 --> 01:21:29.317
And you pull each intersection of that grid down just a few steps.

01:21:30.151 --> 01:21:30.651
All right.

01:21:31.086 --> 01:21:32.153
So then that's the stretch.

01:21:32.988 --> 01:21:34.322
When we age a child,

01:21:34.856 --> 01:21:40.061
of course the first thing we need is a picture of the child and a picture of her father and mother,

01:21:42.030 --> 01:21:44.199
or sibling,

01:21:44.633 --> 01:21:49.271
or whoever the parent or the sibling might be that she looks most like.

01:21:50.739 --> 01:21:56.177
The face can now be merged with the faces of her immediate family to create a composite image.

01:21:57.178 --> 01:21:58.280
With the click of a mouse,

01:21:58.747 --> 01:22:00.916
Barrow brings the missing child's features

01:22:01.116 --> 01:22:01.683
to the front.

01:22:02.918 --> 01:22:04.853
To retain her particular look,

01:22:05.253 --> 01:22:06.755
the eyes and eyebrows,

01:22:09.224 --> 01:22:20.635
we would bring back a little bit of the shape of her nose and a little bit of the shape of her mouth.

01:22:24.439 --> 01:22:25.974
And then we would...

01:22:29.678 --> 01:22:30.412
Bring back

01:22:31.379 --> 01:22:35.016
or bring to the foreground her father's teeth since she is,

01:22:35.650 --> 01:22:36.851
in this particular picture,

01:22:36.851 --> 01:22:38.920
the child had been missing for five years.

01:22:38.987 --> 01:22:45.660
So we were progressing her from five years old to about ten.

01:22:46.394 --> 01:22:48.263
So by that time her teeth would have,

01:22:48.396 --> 01:22:48.964
of course,

01:22:50.165 --> 01:22:53.268
baby teeth would be gone and she would have permanent teeth.

01:22:53.268 --> 01:22:56.237
So we used the father's teeth to put in the picture.

01:22:57.305 --> 01:22:59.541
The computer makes the manipulations easily,

01:23:00.241 --> 01:23:01.142
But Barrow must respond.

01:23:01.142 --> 01:23:04.412
rely on her own judgment to decide which features to alter.

01:23:06.348 --> 01:23:09.484
Will the child look like a parent or more like her sibling?

01:23:11.553 --> 01:23:14.990
It's a question she'll need to answer when aging Donna Martin.

01:23:17.025 --> 01:23:21.663
Creating an image of what she might look like today will pose an extraordinary challenge.

01:23:22.263 --> 01:23:24.833
She'll have to be aged 21 years.

01:23:26.334 --> 01:23:29.571
Donna's face won't be stretched because at age 16,

01:23:29.738 --> 01:23:31.172
her face was fully developed.

01:23:31.206 --> 01:23:31.706
All right.

01:23:32.073 --> 01:23:34.342
Barrow will concentrate on other techniques.

01:23:35.643 --> 01:23:36.143
First,

01:23:36.411 --> 01:23:41.750
photographs of family members close to the age Donna would be today are scanned into the computer.

01:23:42.884 --> 01:23:45.186
Because family members tend to age alike,

01:23:45.620 --> 01:23:49.524
this will help guide Barrow as she makes decisions about aging Donna.

01:23:50.458 --> 01:23:51.760
By studying the photographs,

01:23:52.160 --> 01:23:57.932
Barrow can begin to see at what stage the relatives begin to show crow's feet or lines around the mouth,

01:23:58.433 --> 01:23:59.734
and how severe they might be.

01:24:00.935 --> 01:24:05.240
She can also see when chins begin to droop or when necks begin to sag.

01:24:05.940 --> 01:24:09.544
She really did not look very much at all like her sisters,

01:24:09.611 --> 01:24:11.312
which I expected that she would.

01:24:11.846 --> 01:24:17.052
And she really didn't have a lot of resemblance to her mother.

01:24:18.386 --> 01:24:21.656
So we had no pictures of her father at that age.

01:24:21.756 --> 01:24:22.256
So

01:24:23.024 --> 01:24:26.461
I decided that the best thing to do would be to try to merge her with her mother.

01:24:27.996 --> 01:24:32.267
To merge the image of Donna Martin at 16 with her mother at age 40,

01:24:32.967 --> 01:24:35.970
Barrow must first align and resize the photographs.

01:24:38.139 --> 01:24:40.375
The photograph of Donna is a three-quarter view.

01:24:41.209 --> 01:24:42.444
To align it with her mother's,

01:24:42.844 --> 01:24:45.080
Barrow must adjust it to show a frontal view.

01:24:47.015 --> 01:24:51.352
Now what happens here is that the mother's picture is quite a bit bigger than hers.

01:24:52.220 --> 01:24:58.326
So then what I have to do is size the picture of the mother.

01:24:59.794 --> 01:25:03.898
And that means reducing its size to try to fit the other one.

01:25:20.281 --> 01:25:23.718
Barrow patiently and meticulously merges the photographs.

01:25:24.486 --> 01:25:27.122
She makes sure Donna's features remain dominant,

01:25:27.589 --> 01:25:30.458
while taking some physical cues from her mother's image.

01:25:32.627 --> 01:25:35.797
Then she erases Donna's hairstyle at age 16,

01:25:36.197 --> 01:25:38.166
so a more mature style can be added.

01:25:53.448 --> 01:25:53.948
Finally,

01:25:54.115 --> 01:25:55.850
the transformation is complete.

01:25:56.651 --> 01:25:57.151
The

01:25:57.385 --> 01:26:00.955
16-year-old Donna Martin has become a woman of 37.

01:26:02.957 --> 01:26:03.457
Well,

01:26:03.525 --> 01:26:05.593
it looks a lot like our family.

01:26:06.361 --> 01:26:07.262
Oddly enough,

01:26:08.863 --> 01:26:12.200
it looks like my husband's family rather than myself.

01:26:12.934 --> 01:26:16.471
Geneva Martin will distribute the image of Donna nationwide.

01:26:17.272 --> 01:26:25.914
She hopes it will finally reunite her with her daughter and put to rest that fateful argument that's gone unresolved for 21 years.

01:26:29.918 --> 01:26:34.122
The work of one forensic artist may lead a daughter back to her mother.

01:26:35.256 --> 01:26:37.625
In one of forensic art's most acclaimed cases,

01:26:38.059 --> 01:26:41.896
another artist used his skills to lead police to a killer.

01:26:45.867 --> 01:26:47.335
In 1971,

01:26:47.635 --> 01:26:52.640
the quiet surface of a prosperous community was shattered by a brutal crime.

01:26:53.641 --> 01:26:54.809
A successful accountant,

01:26:55.143 --> 01:26:56.244
John Emil List,

01:26:56.644 --> 01:26:59.047
lived with his family in a mansion in Westfield,

01:26:59.113 --> 01:26:59.614
New Jersey.

01:27:00.682 --> 01:27:02.450
Then his fortunes waned.

01:27:03.751 --> 01:27:06.187
So in November of 1971,

01:27:06.588 --> 01:27:08.289
List began a new life.

01:27:08.957 --> 01:27:09.591
But first,

01:27:09.724 --> 01:27:11.526
he had to end his current one.

01:27:14.395 --> 01:27:16.231
He killed his entire family,

01:27:16.698 --> 01:27:17.232
his wife,

01:27:17.532 --> 01:27:18.166
three children,

01:27:18.433 --> 01:27:19.601
and his elderly mother.

01:27:22.937 --> 01:27:27.609
Then he dragged the bodies into the ballroom and arranged them neatly in a row.

01:27:38.453 --> 01:27:41.656
He tried to clean as much of their blood off the floor as he could.

01:27:47.395 --> 01:27:50.365
He also sat down to write a long letter of confession.

01:27:51.366 --> 01:27:52.100
When he finished,

01:27:52.433 --> 01:27:54.836
he left his house for the last time.

01:28:00.041 --> 01:28:05.580
A month passed before the Westfield police received a call from a woman concerned about her neighbors.

01:28:07.415 --> 01:28:08.683
She hadn't seen them home,

01:28:09.083 --> 01:28:13.955
and their lights had been on day and night until one by one they'd burned out.

01:28:19.227 --> 01:28:19.927
I'm back here.

01:28:19.927 --> 01:28:21.195
I think we're going to have to make entry.

01:28:22.397 --> 01:28:23.498
When the police arrived,

01:28:23.765 --> 01:28:26.634
they found an unattended mansion in need of repair.

01:28:27.769 --> 01:28:31.306
They looked around the outside of the house until they found an open window.

01:28:37.779 --> 01:28:38.379
When they entered,

01:28:38.746 --> 01:28:41.349
they had no idea what was waiting for them.

01:28:43.484 --> 01:28:46.854
The air was cold and filled with the odor of decay.

01:28:49.290 --> 01:28:54.429
They kept searching through the darkness until they finally came upon the List family in the ballroom,

01:28:54.929 --> 01:28:56.297
still neatly laid out,

01:28:57.065 --> 01:28:58.232
dead for a month.

01:29:01.436 --> 01:29:05.373
It was one of the most grotesque murders Westfield police had ever seen.

01:29:07.108 --> 01:29:08.409
When detectives were called in,

01:29:08.743 --> 01:29:12.046
they found the guns List had used and his letter of confession.

01:29:13.314 --> 01:29:17.285
List was an active member in the church and addressed the letter to his pastor.

01:29:18.419 --> 01:29:18.886
In it,

01:29:18.886 --> 01:29:21.823
he explained that he was no longer able to support his family.

01:29:22.223 --> 01:29:25.226
and pay for the mansion his wife had insisted they buy.

01:29:26.961 --> 01:29:30.798
He was worried his family faced a life of humiliation on welfare.

01:29:31.799 --> 01:29:35.903
He was also upset his wife and children had stopped attending church.

01:29:36.704 --> 01:29:39.774
He felt his daughter had taken a misguided interest in acting,

01:29:40.274 --> 01:29:43.611
which threatened to deny her status as one of God's children.

01:29:44.846 --> 01:29:45.380
At least,

01:29:45.380 --> 01:29:46.013
he concluded,

01:29:46.414 --> 01:29:49.050
I'm certain they've all gone to heaven now.

01:29:54.088 --> 01:29:55.356
After slaying his family,

01:29:55.823 --> 01:30:00.428
List left his car at New York's Kennedy Airport and took a bus heading out of town.

01:30:02.130 --> 01:30:03.564
He hid his crime carefully.

01:30:04.565 --> 01:30:07.635
A month passed before anyone noticed his disappearance.

01:30:09.237 --> 01:30:12.373
Police and the FBI conducted a worldwide search,

01:30:12.974 --> 01:30:14.175
but nothing turned up.

01:30:16.511 --> 01:30:17.612
18 years later,

01:30:18.012 --> 01:30:21.482
New Jersey detective Bernard Tracy was still on the case.

01:30:22.450 --> 01:30:31.492
He would make one last effort to find John Emil List by turning to the experts who combine art and science to age the human face.

01:30:32.960 --> 01:30:39.133
An accurate portrait of John List as a middle-aged man might alert someone somewhere to turn him in.

01:30:43.004 --> 01:30:47.074
One thing I can tell you about the John List investigation,

01:30:47.275 --> 01:30:49.544
back in 1971,

01:30:49.544 --> 01:30:51.179
this was a state-of-the-art investigation.

01:30:51.946 --> 01:30:54.949
Tracy was assigned to the case in 1974,

01:30:55.683 --> 01:30:59.053
taking over from detectives who'd been on the case for three years.

01:31:00.354 --> 01:31:04.692
The investigation was one of the most thorough ever conducted by New Jersey police.

01:31:05.793 --> 01:31:06.961
In their search for clues,

01:31:07.295 --> 01:31:11.232
detectives performed psychological profiles of List and his family.

01:31:12.667 --> 01:31:15.937
We did little profiles on the victims.

01:31:17.538 --> 01:31:18.539
That was John List,

01:31:18.573 --> 01:31:20.074
his oldest son.

01:31:21.976 --> 01:31:22.443
His son,

01:31:22.443 --> 01:31:22.943
Frederick,

01:31:24.245 --> 01:31:25.480
was 13 at the time.

01:31:29.383 --> 01:31:30.084
And his daughter,

01:31:30.451 --> 01:31:30.985
Patricia,

01:31:32.954 --> 01:31:33.654
was 16.

01:31:35.590 --> 01:31:38.292
Though the case had cooled by 1974,

01:31:38.693 --> 01:31:42.263
Tracy was convinced John List was still alive.

01:31:43.498 --> 01:31:48.569
The common case is that when a person kills people close to them,

01:31:48.636 --> 01:31:50.771
they kill themselves shortly thereafter.

01:31:51.305 --> 01:31:54.575
If he wasn't found shortly thereafter dead,

01:31:55.376 --> 01:31:57.445
a victim of suicide,

01:31:58.079 --> 01:31:59.981
then I believe he would still be alive.

01:32:00.047 --> 01:32:01.482
It was just a question of where.

01:32:03.818 --> 01:32:05.186
In an effort to find out,

01:32:05.553 --> 01:32:11.125
Tracy persuaded a television producer to include the case in a series on fugitives from justice.

01:32:14.629 --> 01:32:15.229
The producer,

01:32:15.263 --> 01:32:15.763
in turn,

01:32:15.930 --> 01:32:23.604
enlisted the expertise of Philadelphia forensic artist Frank Bender to sculpt the face of List as he might appear at age 63.

01:32:24.772 --> 01:32:28.509
The sculpture would appear on television for millions of people to see.

01:32:29.911 --> 01:32:34.682
If Bender could produce a good likeness of John List 18 years after the crime,

01:32:35.249 --> 01:32:36.851
he might finally be found.

01:32:39.887 --> 01:32:43.391
To help guide his imagination as he adds years to a face,

01:32:43.925 --> 01:32:47.461
Bender carefully reviews the subject's background and his habits,

01:32:48.029 --> 01:32:49.797
knowing they're unlikely to change.

01:32:50.898 --> 01:32:53.200
Behavior can influence how a person ages.

01:32:55.336 --> 01:32:57.004
Before starting the LIST project,

01:32:57.338 --> 01:32:59.307
Bender thoroughly researched his subject.

01:33:00.308 --> 01:33:05.346
He discussed LIST's psychological aspects with forensic psychologists and police experts.

01:33:06.647 --> 01:33:08.349
You mow your lawn in a suit and tie,

01:33:08.349 --> 01:33:09.417
you're not relaxed.

01:33:09.850 --> 01:33:10.184
It's about...

01:33:10.184 --> 01:33:11.786
without staying in control.

01:33:12.119 --> 01:33:14.455
He'll still be wearing probably the same clothes,

01:33:14.455 --> 01:33:15.990
the same type of bow tie.

01:33:15.990 --> 01:33:17.792
None of that's gonna change.

01:33:17.792 --> 01:33:25.232
You know,
I've been going to a church on Sundays and as often as I can to try to get the feel of like what his environment is really like.

01:33:25.266 --> 01:33:31.372
Any clue police gave him about List's life helped Bender form an image of how he may have aged.

01:33:33.174 --> 01:33:33.841
Lifestyle,

01:33:34.775 --> 01:33:35.476
hairstyle,

01:33:36.177 --> 01:33:36.677
hobbies.

01:33:37.578 --> 01:33:38.846
hereditary factors.

01:33:39.113 --> 01:33:53.861
Then I either do my own psychological profile as I've done in numerous cases or as in this case bring in a forensic psychologist and go back and forth with this individual,

01:33:54.528 --> 01:33:56.831
walk around the town for maybe two or three days,

01:33:57.365 --> 01:33:58.799
really get to know this person,

01:33:58.899 --> 01:34:05.339
put my feet in their shoes and in a sense kind of become the person so I can stay one step ahead of them visually.

01:34:08.609 --> 01:34:11.512
To obtain a scientific outlook on the aging process,

01:34:11.979 --> 01:34:15.249
Bender visited the medical school of the University of Pennsylvania.

01:34:19.153 --> 01:34:20.554
Here he consulted with Dr.

01:34:20.621 --> 01:34:21.389
Scott Bartlett,

01:34:21.789 --> 01:34:27.094
an expert in cranial and facial surgery and in repairing congenital deformities of the face.

01:34:28.796 --> 01:34:29.196
Dr.

01:34:29.196 --> 01:34:31.165
Bartlett's knowledge of the aging process,

01:34:31.399 --> 01:34:32.933
as reflected in the human face,

01:34:33.334 --> 01:34:34.869
made him a valuable ally.

01:34:37.004 --> 01:34:41.742
Bender was curious about the appearance of a scar behind List's ear from an operation.

01:34:42.443 --> 01:34:46.380
The scar went from his ear down to at least his collar.

01:34:47.481 --> 01:34:50.918
I would guess that drawing the ear in something like this,

01:34:51.652 --> 01:34:52.987
more to the side of the head,

01:34:53.020 --> 01:34:55.656
and I would guess that there's bound to be,

01:34:55.723 --> 01:34:58.626
if he had an open mastoid operation,

01:34:58.759 --> 01:35:01.896
that he might have a fairly significant scar back here.

01:35:03.564 --> 01:35:03.931
Dr.

01:35:03.931 --> 01:35:06.033
Bartlett pointed out that as List aged,

01:35:06.333 --> 01:35:10.104
wrinkles and folds in his neck could make the scar less noticeable.

01:35:13.441 --> 01:35:17.411
The two men also discussed how aging affects the eyelids and brows,

01:35:17.745 --> 01:35:20.648
which can change significantly over 18 years.

01:35:21.615 --> 01:35:23.184
The canthal attachment here,

01:35:23.217 --> 01:35:25.386
which is the outer and the inner corners of the eye,

01:35:25.553 --> 01:35:26.721
I don't think change a lot.

01:35:26.754 --> 01:35:28.756
I think what we're seeing here is this hooding,

01:35:28.823 --> 01:35:34.995
this laxity of the soft tissue that's now falling over and giving this a downward slant,

01:35:35.229 --> 01:35:42.403
down and outward slant to the eye opening here as opposed to this fresh up and out slant that you see in youth.

01:35:44.205 --> 01:35:44.705
Ultimately,

01:35:45.072 --> 01:35:49.376
Frank Bender would apply his own intuition to what he learned about the case.

01:35:50.511 --> 01:35:53.080
Bender has been a sculptor for more than 30 years,

01:35:53.581 --> 01:35:57.518
showing his work in galleries and museums all over the United States.

01:35:59.053 --> 01:36:03.424
Most of my studies are human behavior.

01:36:03.457 --> 01:36:04.658
I'm a people person.

01:36:04.692 --> 01:36:05.526
When I go to the beach,

01:36:05.626 --> 01:36:10.131
I don't look at the ocean as much as I like studying the people on the beach.

01:36:10.331 --> 01:36:11.198
How they interact,

01:36:11.265 --> 01:36:12.066
what they look like,

01:36:12.133 --> 01:36:12.900
the differences.

01:36:13.167 --> 01:36:15.069
What makes each person different?

01:36:15.136 --> 01:36:15.836
an individual.

01:36:24.612 --> 01:36:26.547
Beginning his sculpture of John List,

01:36:26.914 --> 01:36:27.982
Bender had a problem.

01:36:28.949 --> 01:36:31.118
He had to construct a three-dimensional model,

01:36:31.552 --> 01:36:34.855
yet the only available photograph of List was a frontal view.

01:36:35.923 --> 01:36:38.926
There were no pictures showing a three-quarter or side view.

01:36:40.060 --> 01:36:48.435
Bender would rely on his experience as a commercial photographer to study the photograph and analyze its three dimensions based on highlight and shadow.

01:36:50.171 --> 01:36:51.272
Now with fugitives,

01:36:51.672 --> 01:36:53.073
you have old photographs.

01:36:54.341 --> 01:36:56.143
The most important thing with a fugitive,

01:36:56.410 --> 01:36:56.910
I found,

01:36:57.378 --> 01:37:00.648
is to stay within the perimeters of the skull.

01:37:01.248 --> 01:37:03.617
The same as facial reconstruction.

01:37:03.717 --> 01:37:05.586
This is where the similarities lie.

01:37:06.453 --> 01:37:07.454
If you elongate

01:37:08.255 --> 01:37:08.755
The head.

01:37:09.857 --> 01:37:10.357
You,

01:37:10.391 --> 01:37:11.025
theoretically,

01:37:11.225 --> 01:37:13.027
are changing the shape of the skull,

01:37:13.060 --> 01:37:13.794
the foundation,

01:37:13.928 --> 01:37:14.829
underneath.

01:37:15.963 --> 01:37:17.231
As he worked on his sculpture,

01:37:17.531 --> 01:37:20.501
he checked his work with acetate overlays made from the

01:37:20.868 --> 01:37:21.836
20-year-old photograph.

01:37:23.704 --> 01:37:27.975
I take an old photograph and enlarge it to what I feel would be life-size,

01:37:28.175 --> 01:37:29.710
do acetate overlays,

01:37:30.344 --> 01:37:31.712
and then lie it over.

01:37:33.214 --> 01:37:39.353
the sculpture as I'm working on it to make sure that I'm within the perimeters of the skull.

01:37:41.822 --> 01:37:44.124
As a face began to emerge from clay,

01:37:44.792 --> 01:37:48.462
Bender delved ever deeper into the mind of John List.

01:37:50.631 --> 01:37:56.570
I felt that John List wanted to kind of be,

01:37:58.239 --> 01:37:58.772
in a sense,

01:37:58.772 --> 01:37:59.874
a generic individual.

01:38:01.108 --> 01:38:02.176
You know,
wanted to blend in,

01:38:03.043 --> 01:38:04.178
didn't want to create waves.

01:38:05.679 --> 01:38:07.014
Wanted some peace in his life,

01:38:07.014 --> 01:38:08.482
which he never got from his wife.

01:38:10.851 --> 01:38:13.254
And this started building up his anxiety.

01:38:16.490 --> 01:38:18.726
I really believe he tried to get this through the church,

01:38:19.159 --> 01:38:19.960
and was getting it.

01:38:21.262 --> 01:38:22.329
But when he came back home,

01:38:22.997 --> 01:38:24.064
it was complete overload.

01:38:36.310 --> 01:38:38.512
Once the preliminary sculpture was complete,

01:38:39.046 --> 01:38:41.548
Bender and his assistant made a plaster cast.

01:38:42.149 --> 01:38:42.649
That's good.

01:38:44.518 --> 01:38:45.185
Little by little,

01:38:45.619 --> 01:38:47.254
Bender's sculpture took shape.

01:38:48.055 --> 01:38:48.555
By now,

01:38:48.956 --> 01:38:52.192
Bender was more familiar with List's face than with his own.

01:38:54.428 --> 01:38:57.231
As the bust of the fugitive emerged from its mold,

01:38:57.765 --> 01:39:02.202
Frank Bender was ready to add the final details to the face of a killer.

01:39:05.839 --> 01:39:07.041
John Emil List,

01:39:07.274 --> 01:39:08.542
after murdering his family,

01:39:09.009 --> 01:39:11.378
had evaded capture for 18 years.

01:39:12.446 --> 01:39:18.852
Whether police could find him would depend on Frank Bender's intuitive ability to imagine John List in his early

01:39:19.486 --> 01:39:19.986
60s.

01:39:21.722 --> 01:39:24.925
Bender guessed that List's hairline continued to recede,

01:39:25.292 --> 01:39:27.461
as it had begun to do in his earlier photograph.

01:39:28.996 --> 01:39:32.399
He added jowls based on his assessment of List's lifestyle.

01:39:33.567 --> 01:39:35.970
He wasn't an exercise type person.

01:39:36.070 --> 01:39:37.938
He was a potato and meat type person.

01:39:39.573 --> 01:39:40.841
As far as the eyes,

01:39:40.841 --> 01:39:43.377
you know,
you can see where he's starting to get some lines.

01:39:45.312 --> 01:39:46.613
I look a little tired.

01:39:49.483 --> 01:39:49.883
You see,

01:39:49.883 --> 01:39:50.417
he has,

01:39:50.551 --> 01:39:54.955
I sculpted the tie and the shirt and the coat,

01:39:55.422 --> 01:39:56.523
you know,
the suit coat on him,

01:39:56.523 --> 01:39:58.125
because he's always going to be wearing that.

01:39:58.892 --> 01:40:03.230
Bender sensed that the years of hiding what he had done would take a toll on List.

01:40:03.964 --> 01:40:07.301
This influenced his decision to turn the corners of his mouth down.

01:40:09.436 --> 01:40:12.106
You can see the corners of the mouth going down here,

01:40:12.706 --> 01:40:13.540
rather than up,

01:40:13.707 --> 01:40:14.608
like the photograph,

01:40:14.675 --> 01:40:15.809
which I'll bring right over,

01:40:16.777 --> 01:40:17.678
and you can see.

01:40:19.046 --> 01:40:22.716
How they turned up after 20 years,

01:40:23.984 --> 01:40:25.285
I'm having them go down.

01:40:26.453 --> 01:40:27.888
Not his whole mouth going down,

01:40:27.955 --> 01:40:28.789
just the corners.

01:40:29.656 --> 01:40:30.156
Again,

01:40:31.158 --> 01:40:31.959
not only aging,

01:40:32.059 --> 01:40:34.261
but the fear of getting caught.

01:40:36.663 --> 01:40:37.698
As a final touch,

01:40:38.198 --> 01:40:41.101
Bender selected eyeglasses for the aged Liszt.

01:40:41.969 --> 01:40:46.206
He searched for a style that would define a man who killed his entire family,

01:40:46.740 --> 01:40:48.409
yet called himself a Christian.

01:40:49.777 --> 01:40:52.012
He decided on thick black frames,

01:40:52.579 --> 01:40:54.448
thinking Liszt might go for an austere,

01:40:54.548 --> 01:40:58.852
authoritative look that would discourage others from prying into his past.

01:41:00.320 --> 01:41:03.357
Now his sculpture was ready for the nation to see.

01:41:05.726 --> 01:41:06.193
On May 21st,

01:41:06.193 --> 01:41:08.095
1989,

01:41:08.495 --> 01:41:13.567
millions of people across the United States saw Frank Bender's sculpture of John Emil List.

01:41:14.535 --> 01:41:17.204
Was the likeness good enough to catch the killer?

01:41:18.105 --> 01:41:18.672
Oh my god,

01:41:18.839 --> 01:41:19.640
that's Bob Clark.

01:41:20.274 --> 01:41:23.444
Ricky Yeah?

01:41:23.444 --> 01:41:24.177
Is that Mr.
Clark?

01:41:25.646 --> 01:41:26.346
Yeah,
it looks like it.

01:41:26.346 --> 01:41:28.248
One of the viewers was a woman from Denver,

01:41:28.315 --> 01:41:28.849
Colorado.

01:41:29.850 --> 01:41:30.851
When she saw the bust,

01:41:31.085 --> 01:41:31.785
she called in.

01:41:32.619 --> 01:41:34.721
She said it looked just like her former neighbor,

01:41:35.022 --> 01:41:35.522
Robert P.

01:41:35.689 --> 01:41:36.189
Clark,

01:41:36.323 --> 01:41:37.391
who moved to Virginia.

01:41:42.996 --> 01:41:44.031
The FBI?

01:41:44.031 --> 01:41:46.200
Yes,
can you direct us to the office of Robert Clark,

01:41:46.300 --> 01:41:47.734
please?

01:41:48.335 --> 01:41:48.702
Ah,
yes,

01:41:48.702 --> 01:41:49.303
Mr.
Clark's office.

01:41:49.303 --> 01:41:50.304
Following up on the tip,

01:41:50.804 --> 01:41:55.409
FBI agents found a man who called himself Robert Clark at an office in Richmond,

01:41:55.476 --> 01:41:55.976
Virginia.

01:41:56.977 --> 01:41:59.780
After years of getting away with a horrible murder,

01:42:00.414 --> 01:42:03.951
List was about to see his last days as a free man.

01:42:05.586 --> 01:42:06.787
When the agents approached him,

01:42:07.121 --> 01:42:09.356
he insisted he was not John List.

01:42:10.090 --> 01:42:12.526
But on his neck was the telltale scar,

01:42:13.393 --> 01:42:15.729
and his fingerprints matched those of the killer.

01:42:16.964 --> 01:42:17.464
With that,

01:42:17.731 --> 01:42:21.502
the agents had proof beyond a reasonable doubt that Robert P.

01:42:21.668 --> 01:42:25.639
Clark and John Emil List were in fact the same man.

01:42:27.174 --> 01:42:27.941
Since the murders,

01:42:28.275 --> 01:42:31.845
John List had remarried and recreated his life as an accountant,

01:42:32.446 --> 01:42:37.284
confident that the crimes he'd committed in 1971 would never come back to haunt him.

01:42:38.685 --> 01:42:40.187
For Detective Bernard Tracy,

01:42:40.554 --> 01:42:42.489
it was the end of an incredible odyssey.

01:42:43.223 --> 01:42:47.561
A cold-blooded murderer finally captured after 18 years.

01:42:49.463 --> 01:42:50.197
By all accounts,

01:42:50.564 --> 01:42:56.436
the resemblance between the bust created by Frank Bender and the actual John List was uncanny.

01:42:57.804 --> 01:43:06.079
I think the bust was something that if a person looked at that and had any doubt that that would be the straw that broke the camel's back,

01:43:06.947 --> 01:43:07.781
that would be the one,

01:43:08.048 --> 01:43:12.352
if they had any hesitation about calling and then they saw the bust,

01:43:12.719 --> 01:43:15.122
that would be the one that would take them over to the edge and say,

01:43:15.189 --> 01:43:15.923
I got a call,

01:43:16.523 --> 01:43:17.024
this is him.

01:43:18.458 --> 01:43:25.165
John Emil List was convicted of murdering his family and sentenced to five consecutive life sentences.

01:43:26.266 --> 01:43:33.407
He and his unsuspecting new wife were attending a church function on the night Frank Bender's sculpture aired nationwide.

01:43:34.474 --> 01:43:35.075
As a result,

01:43:35.475 --> 01:43:40.247
they missed the show that gave John List yet another new start in life,

01:43:41.048 --> 01:43:42.983
in New Jersey's State Penitentiary.

01:43:46.019 --> 01:43:47.120
Armed with photographs,

01:43:47.621 --> 01:43:48.422
scientific data,

01:43:49.089 --> 01:43:50.424
psychological profiles,

01:43:50.524 --> 01:43:51.592
and artistic talent,

01:43:52.326 --> 01:43:56.196
forensic artists breathe new life into unsolved cases.

01:43:57.998 --> 01:44:01.702
Using their unique knowledge of human physiology and human nature,

01:44:02.336 --> 01:44:03.971
they can help locate the missing,

01:44:04.805 --> 01:44:06.506
track murderers through time,

01:44:07.207 --> 01:44:09.243
and bring killers to justice.

01:44:17.351 --> 01:44:18.785
Hotel clerk disappears,

01:44:19.186 --> 01:44:20.821
leaving behind a trail of blood.

01:44:23.724 --> 01:44:25.259
Investigators have their suspect,

01:44:25.692 --> 01:44:26.526
but without a victim,

01:44:26.960 --> 01:44:28.228
what can they charge him with?

01:44:34.401 --> 01:44:35.435
A shooting is reported,

01:44:35.836 --> 01:44:38.472
but investigators have no proof that it really occurred.

01:44:40.007 --> 01:44:41.908
Until they can sift fact from fiction,

01:44:42.409 --> 01:44:43.677
they haven't got a case.

01:44:46.813 --> 01:44:49.816
Is a missing sofa the key to a woman's disappearance?

01:44:50.684 --> 01:44:55.422
The prosecution can't rest until it can make sense of a series of odd clues.

01:44:57.591 --> 01:45:00.127
Murder is usually the most obvious of crimes.

01:45:00.727 --> 01:45:01.328
The victims'

01:45:01.361 --> 01:45:03.630
bodies mute witnesses to violence.

01:45:04.631 --> 01:45:10.170
But their silent testimony may go unheard when they disappear without a trace.

01:45:36.930 --> 01:45:37.397
Memphis,

01:45:37.397 --> 01:45:37.898
Tennessee,

01:45:38.365 --> 01:45:39.833
a thriving southern city.

01:45:41.635 --> 01:45:42.569
Home of Beale Street,

01:45:42.936 --> 01:45:43.603
the Blues,

01:45:44.338 --> 01:45:45.238
and Elvis Presley.

01:45:47.574 --> 01:45:48.875
But in 1997,

01:45:49.209 --> 01:45:51.178
it played host to a deadly mystery.

01:45:55.582 --> 01:45:57.751
In the early morning hours of February 8th,

01:45:58.051 --> 01:45:59.920
a man arrived at a Memphis motel.

01:46:05.192 --> 01:46:08.462
He became concerned when calls to the front desk went unanswered.

01:46:09.896 --> 01:46:10.964
He went to check it out.

01:46:13.767 --> 01:46:15.268
The clerk appeared to be missing.

01:46:17.170 --> 01:46:18.572
He didn't see anyone in the lobby,

01:46:18.872 --> 01:46:20.207
but there was blood on the counter.

01:46:25.946 --> 01:46:27.647
The office security door was open.

01:46:28.982 --> 01:46:30.784
And he noticed more blood on the frame.

01:46:33.453 --> 01:46:34.788
Convinced something was wrong,

01:46:35.055 --> 01:46:36.823
he left and called police.

01:46:54.274 --> 01:46:55.709
When
Memphis police arrived,

01:46:55.909 --> 01:46:58.345
they discovered blood outside the motel entrance.

01:46:59.780 --> 01:47:02.816
Smearing indicated something had been dragged across the pavement.

01:47:08.355 --> 01:47:10.290
They found more clues inside.

01:47:14.060 --> 01:47:16.129
While the office showed no sign of forced entry,

01:47:16.563 --> 01:47:18.799
the cash register was open and empty.

01:47:20.567 --> 01:47:24.104
But a purse lying undisturbed nearby caught their attention.

01:47:25.205 --> 01:47:27.941
They didn't believe a woman would willingly leave it behind.

01:47:30.544 --> 01:47:32.679
The wallet inside still contained money,

01:47:33.447 --> 01:47:34.347
as well as a checkbook,

01:47:34.748 --> 01:47:35.382
credit cards,

01:47:35.582 --> 01:47:38.084
and driver's license in the name of Ricky Ellsworth.

01:47:40.487 --> 01:47:41.421
Alerted by police,

01:47:41.788 --> 01:47:46.560
the motel manager arrived and confirmed that Ricky Ellsworth was the clerk on duty.

01:47:48.328 --> 01:47:50.497
She said Ricky lived nearby with her husband,

01:47:50.664 --> 01:47:51.164
Don,

01:47:51.164 --> 01:47:51.932
and two children.

01:47:53.066 --> 01:47:53.600
A trusted,

01:47:53.767 --> 01:47:54.768
reliable employee,

01:47:55.168 --> 01:47:58.171
she wasn't the sort to just leave in the middle of her shift.

01:48:00.040 --> 01:48:02.976
Circumstances of the crime suggested more than robbery.

01:48:03.910 --> 01:48:06.112
Police suspected the clerk had been abducted.

01:48:09.149 --> 01:48:10.584
As police processed the scene,

01:48:10.884 --> 01:48:13.119
other significant details began to emerge.

01:48:14.588 --> 01:48:17.190
The office security door was equipped with a keypad,

01:48:17.724 --> 01:48:20.227
but it was open and showed no sign of tampering.

01:48:21.261 --> 01:48:22.863
Either someone had known the code,

01:48:23.363 --> 01:48:25.031
or the clerk had unlocked the door.

01:48:26.233 --> 01:48:27.033
To investigators,

01:48:27.400 --> 01:48:30.403
that suggested Ricky Ellsworth might have known her abductor.

01:48:33.607 --> 01:48:35.976
Although police had begun to suspect foul play,

01:48:36.610 --> 01:48:39.880
nothing prepared them for what they found in an adjacent bathroom.

01:48:42.115 --> 01:48:45.886
There were signs of a desperate struggle and much more blood.

01:48:47.287 --> 01:48:49.689
The running sink contained a small flashlight.

01:48:51.091 --> 01:48:53.293
and the toilet seat had been ripped from its hinges.

01:48:54.327 --> 01:48:54.794
Wet,

01:48:54.794 --> 01:48:58.098
blood-tinged sheets indicated someone had tried to clean the room.

01:49:00.367 --> 01:49:02.536
Not only had Rikki Ellsworth disappeared,

01:49:02.936 --> 01:49:05.338
but it now seemed she'd been seriously injured.

01:49:07.140 --> 01:49:07.807
Captain R.G.

01:49:08.041 --> 01:49:08.541
Moore,

01:49:08.575 --> 01:49:09.943
who led the crime scene unit,

01:49:10.310 --> 01:49:11.311
feared the worst.

01:49:12.879 --> 01:49:15.415
When we don't have a body and we don't know exactly what happened,

01:49:15.448 --> 01:49:17.884
we'd handle it just like it's going to be a homicide case.

01:49:18.718 --> 01:49:20.820
We process the scene,

01:49:20.820 --> 01:49:22.022
we collect our blood sample,

01:49:22.022 --> 01:49:27.160
we collect all the evidence we can find from the scene and just keep it until we see what we've got.

01:49:29.229 --> 01:49:31.765
Investigators hoped the evidence would lead them to Ricky.

01:49:35.969 --> 01:49:38.004
A squad car brought the victim's husband,

01:49:38.104 --> 01:49:38.872
Don Ellsworth,

01:49:38.972 --> 01:49:39.639
to the motel.

01:49:45.445 --> 01:49:47.547
He said he and Ricky were happily married.

01:49:49.783 --> 01:49:55.355
He told police his wife was a kind person who worked for the Christian prison ministry in her spare time.

01:49:56.456 --> 01:49:57.090
Every Christmas,

01:49:57.357 --> 01:49:59.159
she baked pies for the prisoners.

01:50:01.494 --> 01:50:04.397
But Ellsworth said their marriage hadn't always been so tranquil.

01:50:07.300 --> 01:50:09.069
They'd once separated for two years,

01:50:09.436 --> 01:50:13.173
and she'd gotten involved with a man who assaulted her and went to prison for it.

01:50:14.774 --> 01:50:17.210
Ellsworth said the man's name was Michael Rimmer.

01:50:20.714 --> 01:50:21.948
After finishing at the scene,

01:50:22.248 --> 01:50:26.486
the investigators returned to the station to conduct a background check on Michael Rimmer.

01:50:29.289 --> 01:50:31.591
What they found in the files was chilling.

01:50:35.095 --> 01:50:39.265
Rimmer was a high school dropout with a history of drug problems and violence.

01:50:40.166 --> 01:50:41.601
In 1989,

01:50:41.601 --> 01:50:45.138
he was convicted of robbing and brutally assaulting Ricky Ellsworth.

01:50:46.539 --> 01:50:51.645
Details of Rimmer's previous assault on Ricky raised Detective Robert Shenwell's suspicions.

01:50:52.612 --> 01:51:00.754
His actions at that crime basically mirrored what was happening here at the motel with the cleaning up,

01:51:01.021 --> 01:51:01.921
or attempt to clean up,

01:51:01.988 --> 01:51:02.656
the evidence.

01:51:04.157 --> 01:51:06.292
We immediately began looking for Michael Rimmer.

01:51:07.627 --> 01:51:08.161
Meanwhile,

01:51:08.395 --> 01:51:11.531
detectives narrowed the time frame for Ricky's disappearance.

01:51:12.265 --> 01:51:15.402
They found a couple who had checked in with her at 1.15 a.m.,

01:51:16.336 --> 01:51:19.072
but when two others tried to check out around 2.30,

01:51:19.472 --> 01:51:20.240
she wasn't there.

01:51:25.378 --> 01:51:29.549
Detectives located a witness who said he pulled into the motel at approximately 2.15,

01:51:29.949 --> 01:51:30.950
hoping to rent a room.

01:51:36.423 --> 01:51:41.194
But he saw a man with bloody knuckles behind the counter and left without registering.

01:51:43.463 --> 01:51:47.267
He also noticed a four-door maroon car backed up to the curb,

01:51:47.667 --> 01:51:49.269
its trunk and doors open.

01:51:51.938 --> 01:51:53.006
Based on his description,

01:51:53.373 --> 01:51:56.009
police put together a composite of the man at the motel.

01:51:57.977 --> 01:51:58.878
Using an identikit,

01:51:59.312 --> 01:52:02.682
a collection of facial parts and features that can be assembled as needed,

01:52:03.216 --> 01:52:04.951
the suspect's face came together.

01:52:05.852 --> 01:52:07.587
It resembled Michael Rimmer.

01:52:09.689 --> 01:52:13.793
Detectives then asked the witness to look through an album of more than 50 mugshots,

01:52:14.294 --> 01:52:15.395
including Rimmer's.

01:52:16.463 --> 01:52:21.835
Their hopes were dashed when he couldn't positively identify Rimmer as the man with the bloody knuckles.

01:52:23.970 --> 01:52:29.743
Detectives learned from a co-worker that Ricky had recently gotten a birthday card from a Michael in Mississippi.

01:52:32.846 --> 01:52:35.048
She took the card into the back office to read,

01:52:35.515 --> 01:52:37.317
but became angry and threw it away.

01:52:42.155 --> 01:52:46.126
It was becoming clear to detectives that if they wanted to find Ricky Ellsworth,

01:52:46.559 --> 01:52:48.428
they needed to find Michael Rimmer.

01:52:49.696 --> 01:52:52.165
They started at the auto repair shop where he worked.

01:52:53.066 --> 01:52:54.667
Nobody had seen him since Friday,

01:52:55.235 --> 01:52:57.170
the day before Ricky disappeared.

01:52:58.404 --> 01:53:01.007
He'd left his tools and paycheck behind.

01:53:02.475 --> 01:53:04.577
One more critical fact emerged.

01:53:05.879 --> 01:53:07.981
When we got to talking to the employees there,

01:53:09.282 --> 01:53:13.586
this same maroon four-door vehicle was described as being Michael Rimmer's car.

01:53:15.088 --> 01:53:20.260
So then we decided we need to track down and find out where Michael Rimmer got this four-door maroon vehicle.

01:53:20.894 --> 01:53:22.362
It wasn't registered to Rimmer.

01:53:23.329 --> 01:53:24.197
If it wasn't his,

01:53:24.531 --> 01:53:26.232
detectives wondered where he'd gotten it.

01:53:27.433 --> 01:53:34.474
His co-workers sent detectives to another friend of Rimmer's who told them that his own maroon four-door car had been stolen a month earlier,

01:53:35.241 --> 01:53:37.377
shortly after he'd last seen Rimmer.

01:53:40.814 --> 01:53:41.781
After four days,

01:53:42.215 --> 01:53:43.917
Ricky Ellsworth was still missing,

01:53:44.584 --> 01:53:46.085
and so was Michael Rimmer.

01:53:49.355 --> 01:53:51.090
Hoping for a clue to his whereabouts,

01:53:51.391 --> 01:53:52.525
police questioned his brother.

01:53:53.693 --> 01:53:57.297
Richard Rimmer said he'd last seen Michael on the morning of February 9th,

01:53:57.897 --> 01:54:00.600
just hours after Ricky Ellsworth disappeared.

01:54:06.539 --> 01:54:11.010
Michael had seemed exhausted when he arrived in the maroon car around 9.30 a.m.

01:54:13.746 --> 01:54:17.150
He asked Richard if he knew how to remove a blood stain from the back seat.

01:54:20.320 --> 01:54:20.820
Then,

01:54:20.954 --> 01:54:25.091
Michael pulled a muddy shovel from the car and scraped mud from his boots.

01:54:27.861 --> 01:54:31.798
Richard also told police that Michael claimed Ricky bought the car for him.

01:54:33.399 --> 01:54:37.337
She had visited Rimmer in prison after he claimed he'd found God.

01:54:39.606 --> 01:54:41.941
And Richard knew the two had met after his release.

01:54:42.775 --> 01:54:44.377
But somewhere along the line,

01:54:44.711 --> 01:54:45.912
the friendship soured.

01:54:49.082 --> 01:54:49.582
By now,

01:54:49.983 --> 01:54:50.917
two weeks had passed,

01:54:51.150 --> 01:54:54.187
and still nobody had seen or heard from Ricky Ellsworth.

01:54:55.655 --> 01:54:58.191
Detectives had lost hope of finding her alive,

01:54:58.725 --> 01:54:59.759
and their prime suspect,

01:55:00.059 --> 01:55:00.660
Michael Rimmer,

01:55:01.060 --> 01:55:01.861
still eluded them.

01:55:02.896 --> 01:55:08.201
They would have to find him in the stolen car if they had any chance of proving murder.

01:55:09.002 --> 01:55:17.911
I issued a theft of property warrant on Michael Rimmer and indicated in that warrant that when he was located,

01:55:19.045 --> 01:55:20.413
or if that vehicle was located,

01:55:20.446 --> 01:55:24.050
that it was to be held for the Memphis Homicide Unit and processed.

01:55:26.085 --> 01:55:29.622
Detectives got a promising lead from one of Rimmer's former cellmates.

01:55:30.924 --> 01:55:35.962
He said Rimmer spoke of killing Ricky and burying her in Mississippi after he got out of prison.

01:55:38.264 --> 01:55:45.238
Rimmer's former girlfriend told detectives of a wooded area called Plantation Point near Arca Butler Lake in Mississippi.

01:55:46.005 --> 01:55:47.907
It was 45 minutes from Memphis.

01:55:48.741 --> 01:55:50.476
She said Rimmer liked to go there.

01:55:52.412 --> 01:55:54.180
Detectives headed to Plantation Point.

01:55:54.981 --> 01:55:56.516
They combed the area on foot,

01:55:57.016 --> 01:55:58.184
but didn't find anything.

01:56:00.586 --> 01:56:04.090
Then they called in black water divers from the Shelby County Surge.

01:56:04.157 --> 01:56:04.924
...and rescue unit.

01:56:06.693 --> 01:56:09.395
Specially trained to operate by touch in dark,

01:56:09.696 --> 01:56:10.196
murky,

01:56:10.196 --> 01:56:10.863
black water,

01:56:11.431 --> 01:56:12.832
the divers searched the lake.

01:56:14.467 --> 01:56:15.902
They didn't find anything either.

01:56:18.838 --> 01:56:20.540
Detectives were thoroughly frustrated.

01:56:22.108 --> 01:56:24.377
They'd chased down every witness and lead,

01:56:24.811 --> 01:56:29.882
but were no closer to solving the case or finding Ricky Ellsworth or Michael Rimmer.

01:56:31.451 --> 01:56:31.951
Now,

01:56:32.118 --> 01:56:32.618
they'd...

01:56:35.488 --> 01:56:36.022
In Memphis,

01:56:36.389 --> 01:56:40.159
nearly a month had passed with no progress in the potential homicide case.

01:56:41.861 --> 01:56:42.562
In early March,

01:56:42.895 --> 01:56:45.131
detectives got a phone call from Johnson County,

01:56:45.298 --> 01:56:45.798
Indiana.

01:56:48.001 --> 01:56:51.170
A sheriff's deputy there had stopped a maroon car for speeding.

01:56:52.405 --> 01:56:54.140
When he ran a check on the license plate,

01:56:54.474 --> 01:56:55.942
he discovered the car was stolen,

01:56:56.409 --> 01:56:57.977
and there was a warrant for the driver.

01:56:59.412 --> 01:57:00.079
Michael Rimmer,

01:57:00.546 --> 01:57:03.282
sought for crimes including robbery and possible murder,

01:57:03.716 --> 01:57:06.319
had been arrested during a routine traffic stop.

01:57:07.754 --> 01:57:09.122
Detectives flew to Indiana,

01:57:09.522 --> 01:57:13.192
prepared to finally question Rimmer and examine the car for evidence.

01:57:13.826 --> 01:57:14.894
After obtaining a warrant,

01:57:15.294 --> 01:57:18.831
forensic investigators with the Johnson County Crime Unit searched the car.

01:57:20.233 --> 01:57:21.801
They hoped for solid evidence,

01:57:22.235 --> 01:57:24.704
but never expected to find what they did.

01:57:25.772 --> 01:57:26.806
Numerous receipts,

01:57:27.673 --> 01:57:28.674
hotel receipts,

01:57:29.075 --> 01:57:30.376
pawn shop receipts,

01:57:31.144 --> 01:57:32.045
restaurant receipts,

01:57:33.479 --> 01:57:33.979
showing

01:57:34.680 --> 01:57:40.353
Mike's every step from the day Ricky came up missing to his actual apprehension.

01:57:41.154 --> 01:57:44.390
Investigators tested the stained back seat for human blood.

01:57:45.558 --> 01:57:46.692
Results were positive.

01:57:47.527 --> 01:57:52.165
It was their first physical evidence to suggest a connection between Rimmer and the crime.

01:57:54.600 --> 01:57:57.970
Memphis detectives questioned Michael Rimmer over the next two days.

01:57:59.472 --> 01:58:03.776
He denied stealing the car or having anything to do with Ricky's disappearance.

01:58:06.045 --> 01:58:06.546
So far,

01:58:06.946 --> 01:58:08.548
all their evidence was circumstantial.

01:58:10.950 --> 01:58:11.951
Without Ricky's body,

01:58:12.351 --> 01:58:15.455
there was only one way to link Rimmer to her murder.

01:58:19.492 --> 01:58:24.964
Blood from the rear of the vehicle Michael Rimmer was occupying and the blood from the scene matched.

01:58:27.100 --> 01:58:27.800
We had a problem,

01:58:27.867 --> 01:58:29.402
though.
We had to determine,

01:58:30.369 --> 01:58:32.972
show somehow that that blood was Ricky Ellsworth's blood.

01:58:34.540 --> 01:58:35.842
They had one chance,

01:58:36.342 --> 01:58:38.411
a reverse paternity DNA test,

01:58:38.644 --> 01:58:44.917
which uses the combined DNA traits passed on from parents to determine the DNA characteristics of their children.

01:58:46.953 --> 01:58:52.859
Forensic scientists compared blood from Ricky's mother to samples from the crime scene and the maroon car.

01:58:55.194 --> 01:58:55.828
They matched.

01:58:56.929 --> 01:59:05.171
The test established a 16 million to 1 probability that blood from the car and motel came from an offspring of Ricky's mother.

01:59:06.139 --> 01:59:09.375
It was the final link police needed to prove murder.

01:59:10.343 --> 01:59:12.879
Michael Rimmer's guilt was written in blood.

01:59:15.381 --> 01:59:16.182
Based on evidence,

01:59:16.482 --> 01:59:19.218
police believe they know what happened on February 8th.

01:59:22.488 --> 01:59:26.159
Michael Rimmer drove to the motel sometime around 1.30 a.m.

01:59:26.759 --> 01:59:28.761
knowing Ricky Ellsworth would be alone.

01:59:34.400 --> 01:59:35.234
When he arrived,

01:59:35.568 --> 01:59:36.802
she let him into the office.

01:59:42.508 --> 01:59:43.008
There,

01:59:43.209 --> 01:59:44.977
Rimmer exploded into violence,

01:59:45.344 --> 01:59:45.878
attacking her,

01:59:46.245 --> 01:59:47.947
then wrapping her body in sheets.

01:59:53.452 --> 01:59:53.952
Afterward,

01:59:54.053 --> 01:59:55.188
he tried to clean the room,

01:59:55.755 --> 01:59:57.790
but gave up and left the sink running.

02:00:00.760 --> 02:00:02.228
Dragging Ricky to his car,

02:00:02.728 --> 02:00:03.930
Rimmer made his escape.

02:00:04.630 --> 02:00:06.265
Although her body was never found,

02:00:06.699 --> 02:00:10.703
police believe Ricky Ellsworth is buried somewhere near Arca Butler Lake.

02:00:13.739 --> 02:00:17.677
Michael Rimmer was convicted of first-degree murder and sentenced to death.

02:00:21.480 --> 02:00:24.083
While no body was ever found in the Rimmer case,

02:00:24.483 --> 02:00:26.686
police used blood evidence to prove murder.

02:00:32.124 --> 02:00:32.825
But in Cleveland,

02:00:32.892 --> 02:00:33.392
Ohio,

02:00:33.693 --> 02:00:35.995
police would have to build a case on even less.

02:00:38.064 --> 02:00:38.631
On Saturday,

02:00:38.731 --> 02:00:39.532
December 15,

02:00:39.532 --> 02:00:41.434
1984,

02:00:41.434 --> 02:00:44.270
Cleveland police were called to the home of Ernestine Campbell.

02:00:46.539 --> 02:00:47.673
She reported that her brother,

02:00:47.773 --> 02:00:48.241
Henry,

02:00:48.241 --> 02:00:48.741
was missing,

02:00:49.308 --> 02:00:50.509
and she feared for his life,

02:00:50.810 --> 02:00:54.146
because she'd heard he'd been shot and his body hidden in an alley.

02:00:57.917 --> 02:01:00.620
She told police she'd last seen Henry the night before,

02:01:00.987 --> 02:01:03.522
when he'd left home for a nearby after-hour spot.

02:01:11.764 --> 02:01:14.433
Officers searched the alley but didn't find a body.

02:01:15.268 --> 02:01:15.768
In fact,

02:01:15.968 --> 02:01:18.204
they didn't find any signs of foul play.

02:01:20.439 --> 02:01:23.476
They drove through the neighborhood but didn't see anything unusual.

02:01:26.045 --> 02:01:30.883
No sign of Henry Campbell and nothing to indicate a violent crime that occurred.

02:01:32.618 --> 02:01:33.986
Officers canvassed the area,

02:01:34.387 --> 02:01:37.623
talking to residents who might have seen or heard something the night before.

02:01:40.026 --> 02:01:43.863
One witness claimed she heard shots and saw men running near the club.

02:01:46.032 --> 02:01:47.466
Another recalled hearing shots,

02:01:47.800 --> 02:01:49.568
but couldn't provide any other details.

02:01:50.469 --> 02:01:52.872
Still others confirmed shots were fired,

02:01:53.306 --> 02:01:55.174
but nobody admitted seeing anything.

02:01:56.876 --> 02:01:57.843
Police were frustrated.

02:01:58.411 --> 02:02:00.446
They had reports of gunfire and a murder,

02:02:00.813 --> 02:02:02.615
but couldn't find the purported victim.

02:02:05.051 --> 02:02:06.652
Homicide detective John Qualey,

02:02:07.019 --> 02:02:08.020
the lead investigator,

02:02:08.354 --> 02:02:10.790
recalls the odd circumstances of the case.

02:02:12.024 --> 02:02:17.396
Usually we're called to the scene of a homicide where we have a victim laying on the street or in a house,

02:02:17.630 --> 02:02:18.731
but we have a victim.

02:02:19.965 --> 02:02:24.203
And a lot of times we'll be able to get the weapon used in a crime.

02:02:24.470 --> 02:02:25.338
with this incident.

02:02:26.672 --> 02:02:27.606
We were,

02:02:27.673 --> 02:02:29.208
a murder was reported,

02:02:29.208 --> 02:02:30.109
we went to the scene,

02:02:30.109 --> 02:02:31.277
we didn't have a body.

02:02:31.877 --> 02:02:33.679
We couldn't find the body,

02:02:33.779 --> 02:02:35.348
we couldn't find any evidence of blood,

02:02:36.048 --> 02:02:37.316
or we didn't have a weapon.

02:02:38.818 --> 02:02:42.121
It was kind of starting without anything to work with.

02:02:46.325 --> 02:02:47.827
Police needed more information,

02:02:48.060 --> 02:02:49.695
so they returned to the Campbell house.

02:02:51.364 --> 02:02:54.600
Ernestine gave them a picture of Henry and more details.

02:02:57.937 --> 02:03:02.441
She'd heard that some people claimed they'd seen Clarence Barnes shoot her brother in the back.

02:03:04.744 --> 02:03:10.116
She said Barnes ran the after-hours club in his home and that there was bad blood between him and Henry.

02:03:14.053 --> 02:03:14.653
At the station,

02:03:14.987 --> 02:03:17.790
people who had been at the club that night began coming forward.

02:03:19.425 --> 02:03:25.398
A woman named Jacqueline Richardson told investigators she saw Campbell at the club and that he argued with Barnes.

02:03:36.442 --> 02:03:37.376
When Campbell left,

02:03:37.877 --> 02:03:38.377
Barnes,

02:03:38.477 --> 02:03:39.145
who had a gun,

02:03:39.545 --> 02:03:40.613
followed him outside.

02:03:51.290 --> 02:03:54.894
She said the two men were joined by a third and walked out of her sight.

02:04:04.637 --> 02:04:05.438
Shortly after that,

02:04:05.671 --> 02:04:07.006
a woman ran into the club,

02:04:07.339 --> 02:04:08.574
screaming that there'd been a shooting,

02:04:08.941 --> 02:04:10.376
and Henry Campbell was injured.

02:04:13.245 --> 02:04:14.513
Richardson went outside,

02:04:14.747 --> 02:04:18.217
where a passerby told her some men were chasing and shooting at another.

02:04:20.119 --> 02:04:21.520
She said she didn't see anything,

02:04:21.887 --> 02:04:22.888
and returned to the club.

02:04:24.056 --> 02:04:25.224
Barnes was already there,

02:04:25.791 --> 02:04:27.059
asking everyone to leave.

02:04:29.195 --> 02:04:31.363
As the search continued for evidence of murder,

02:04:31.831 --> 02:04:34.099
Victor Santiago told police what he knew.

02:04:37.236 --> 02:04:40.105
He was the third man Jacqueline Richardson had described,

02:04:40.473 --> 02:04:42.741
and his account took up where hers left off.

02:04:48.747 --> 02:04:54.453
He said he'd seen the two men arguing outside the club and heard Barnes order Campbell to leave.

02:04:56.655 --> 02:04:57.656
According to Santiago,

02:04:58.157 --> 02:05:00.125
Campbell caught up with him a few moments later.

02:05:03.562 --> 02:05:04.730
As they walked down the street,

02:05:04.997 --> 02:05:07.399
he heard a shot and Campbell fell forward.

02:05:08.434 --> 02:05:09.869
Santiago said he took cover,

02:05:10.402 --> 02:05:13.873
then watched Barnes fire three more shots at the prostrate Campbell.

02:05:16.876 --> 02:05:19.078
Police now had two eyewitness accounts,

02:05:19.512 --> 02:05:22.414
but still no evidence of a homicide.

02:05:25.985 --> 02:05:30.523
Henry Campbell had disappeared and detectives had two eyewitness accounts of murder,

02:05:30.923 --> 02:05:33.259
but no body and no evidence.

02:05:35.027 --> 02:05:41.267
Several residents claimed Clarence Barnes bragged that he burned Campbell's body and that nobody would ever find him.

02:05:44.737 --> 02:05:47.540
Investigators brought Barnes to the station for questioning.

02:05:54.680 --> 02:05:57.650
He admitted having words with Campbell and asking him to leave,

02:05:58.017 --> 02:05:59.985
but said he didn't know what happened after that.

02:06:02.421 --> 02:06:04.089
He denied having anything to do with it.

02:06:04.123 --> 02:06:05.591
with Campbell's disappearance.

02:06:07.092 --> 02:06:09.295
When police asked Barnes what he did for a living,

02:06:09.728 --> 02:06:13.132
he told them he worked at the Animal Resource Center at a local university.

02:06:16.201 --> 02:06:18.804
That immediately rang a bell in Qualey's mind,

02:06:19.171 --> 02:06:20.105
and he called Barnes'

02:06:20.239 --> 02:06:20.839
supervisor.

02:06:22.641 --> 02:06:26.745
We asked him if he had an incinerator in the school.

02:06:27.346 --> 02:06:27.813
And he said,

02:06:27.813 --> 02:06:28.347
oh,
yes.

02:06:28.347 --> 02:06:31.183
And we asked him how big it was.

02:06:32.017 --> 02:06:34.286
He said it was big enough to incinerate a steer.

02:06:35.788 --> 02:06:44.563
We told him then that we were investigating a homicide and that possibly there may be human remains in the incinerator and asked him if he would seal the room.

02:06:45.197 --> 02:06:45.697
He did.

02:06:47.700 --> 02:06:48.434
The next morning,

02:06:48.801 --> 02:06:52.738
detectives and a team of forensic investigators went to the Animal Resource Center,

02:06:53.205 --> 02:06:55.808
believing they might find the remains of Henry Campbell.

02:06:57.309 --> 02:07:00.346
While the facility director arranged access to the incinerator,

02:07:00.779 --> 02:07:05.050
the security chief showed them surveillance video from the night Campbell disappeared.

02:07:07.152 --> 02:07:10.022
It showed Barnes arriving around 3.30 a.m.

02:07:10.823 --> 02:07:12.524
He backed into the receiving area,

02:07:12.825 --> 02:07:16.161
which was approximately 15 feet from the incinerator entrance.

02:07:18.230 --> 02:07:21.266
Police could see him carrying something toward the incinerator room,

02:07:21.667 --> 02:07:23.602
but couldn't identify what it was.

02:07:24.970 --> 02:07:32.978
The picture then jumped away and came back and the next picture was the incinerator door open and Clarence pushing a cart in there.

02:07:32.978 --> 02:07:37.416
You could not see what was on the cart but we knew he was in the incinerator room at that hour.

02:07:39.618 --> 02:07:44.723
When investigators saw the incinerator they knew they were in for a long complicated task.

02:07:48.560 --> 02:07:48.927
Dr.

02:07:48.927 --> 02:07:50.029
Elizabeth Balraj,

02:07:50.329 --> 02:07:51.764
coroner of Cuyahoga County,

02:07:52.231 --> 02:07:53.432
recalls the challenge.

02:07:54.299 --> 02:08:02.908
There were 100 gallons of cremated ashes containing remains from various animals.

02:08:03.475 --> 02:08:09.081
And we had to sift through those and find a very small amount of human bones.

02:08:10.349 --> 02:08:12.785
If human bones were to be found at all,

02:08:15.721 --> 02:08:20.025
the team began the painstaking process of sifting through the incinerator contents.

02:08:21.660 --> 02:08:23.228
Using a fine mesh screen,

02:08:23.629 --> 02:08:26.799
they collected fragments of bone and other unburned debris.

02:08:28.367 --> 02:08:30.703
Though most of the fragments were tiny and charred,

02:08:31.103 --> 02:08:32.271
some appeared to be human,

02:08:32.771 --> 02:08:33.572
including a vertebra,

02:08:33.972 --> 02:08:34.472
mandible,

02:08:34.707 --> 02:08:35.741
and part of a pelvis.

02:08:37.976 --> 02:08:40.012
They also found a piece of melted lead.

02:08:42.715 --> 02:08:46.452
Its size and mass were consistent with a .38 caliber bullet.

02:08:47.352 --> 02:08:49.688
It was the first solid evidence of murder.

02:08:52.257 --> 02:08:55.027
Although investigators believed they'd found Henry Campbell,

02:08:55.494 --> 02:08:58.263
their case would go up in smoke if they couldn't prove it.

02:09:00.132 --> 02:09:01.066
They turned to Dr.

02:09:01.100 --> 02:09:01.500
C.

02:09:01.500 --> 02:09:02.334
Owen Lovejoy,

02:09:02.701 --> 02:09:09.174
a biological anthropologist at Kent State University and an expert at identifying altered or damaged remains.

02:09:10.609 --> 02:09:17.015
He applied his skills to making a positive identification from the incomplete and severely damaged bone fragments.

02:09:18.751 --> 02:09:20.152
Lovejoy concurred with Dr.

02:09:20.219 --> 02:09:24.523
Balraj that approximately 160 of the recovered bones were human.

02:09:25.557 --> 02:09:30.963
The next step was determining whether they came from one person and whether that person was Henry Campbell.

02:09:32.364 --> 02:09:34.833
The process would be complicated by their condition.

02:09:35.701 --> 02:09:37.102
Here are the pieces of the puzzle.

02:09:37.770 --> 02:09:39.338
All of the edges had been frayed,

02:09:39.738 --> 02:09:40.238
as you will,

02:09:40.305 --> 02:09:42.040
by the cremation process.

02:09:42.608 --> 02:09:47.379
And so none of the pieces fit together and we had to look at them in an isolated state.

02:09:48.714 --> 02:09:50.649
After a thorough inventory of the fragments,

02:09:50.983 --> 02:09:54.353
Lovejoy determined that portions of an entire skeleton were present.

02:09:55.854 --> 02:09:57.289
No bones were duplicated.

02:09:58.323 --> 02:10:01.627
That told Lovejoy the remains came from a single individual.

02:10:03.695 --> 02:10:05.164
But was it Henry Campbell?

02:10:06.598 --> 02:10:07.032
Next,

02:10:07.032 --> 02:10:09.835
he would try to establish specifics about the individual.

02:10:10.869 --> 02:10:13.005
Investigators had recovered part of the pelvis,

02:10:13.472 --> 02:10:15.974
which is diagnostic for both sex and age.

02:10:17.042 --> 02:10:22.314
Lovejoy concluded the remains belonged to a male approximately Henry Campbell's age.

02:10:23.282 --> 02:10:26.084
While detectives were closer to proving they'd found Campbell,

02:10:26.552 --> 02:10:28.220
they needed positive identification.

02:10:29.388 --> 02:10:31.123
But Lovejoy couldn't give them that,

02:10:31.857 --> 02:10:33.392
not without more to go on.

02:10:34.026 --> 02:10:41.867
Whenever we have a candidate individual that we believe the skeletal remains might match,

02:10:42.534 --> 02:10:45.103
what we do is we try to get a premortem x-ray.

02:10:46.672 --> 02:10:50.309
Investigators delivered existing x-rays of Henry Campbell to Dr.

02:10:50.375 --> 02:10:50.876
Lovejoy.

02:10:52.778 --> 02:10:54.613
Could they help him prove murder?

02:10:58.116 --> 02:11:02.020
As Cleveland detectives sought to identify the bones found in the incinerator,

02:11:02.521 --> 02:11:02.888
Dr.

02:11:02.888 --> 02:11:07.926
Owen Lovejoy compared post-mortem x-rays and bone fragments with Campbell's radiographs.

02:11:08.727 --> 02:11:09.862
He focused on the skull,

02:11:10.329 --> 02:11:10.762
hand,

02:11:10.762 --> 02:11:11.430
and mandible.

02:11:12.764 --> 02:11:15.767
One recovered cranial fragment had a BB embedded in it.

02:11:16.702 --> 02:11:21.540
That matched an x-ray taken when Henry Campbell had accidentally been shot years earlier.

02:11:22.608 --> 02:11:29.014
Lovejoy also identified shared characteristics between Campbell's other x-rays and corresponding bone fragments.

02:11:29.915 --> 02:11:33.318
But he found the most compelling correlations in the lower mandible,

02:11:33.719 --> 02:11:34.519
or jawbone.

02:11:35.020 --> 02:11:36.421
And the outline of that jaw,

02:11:36.722 --> 02:11:38.657
the details of the bone structure,

02:11:39.224 --> 02:11:44.329
and the details of the root areas where the teeth had been prior to death,

02:11:44.997 --> 02:11:45.497
all

02:11:45.864 --> 02:11:49.902
were configured in such a way that they indicated that this was a positive identification.

02:11:51.103 --> 02:11:54.239
Since the jaw figured so prominently in that identification,

02:11:54.640 --> 02:11:54.973
Dr.

02:11:54.973 --> 02:11:55.941
Elizabeth Robinson,

02:11:56.141 --> 02:11:57.876
the team's forensic odontologist,

02:11:58.243 --> 02:11:59.912
reviewed Lovejoy's conclusions.

02:12:01.847 --> 02:12:02.547
In her opinion,

02:12:02.981 --> 02:12:05.684
two significant findings bolstered his analysis.

02:12:08.520 --> 02:12:10.222
The end of the jawbone was flat,

02:12:10.856 --> 02:12:13.458
a unique defect present in Henry Campbell's jaw.

02:12:14.493 --> 02:12:15.360
A second anomaly,

02:12:15.761 --> 02:12:17.462
an area of intense calcification,

02:12:18.063 --> 02:12:19.264
clinched the identification.

02:12:20.332 --> 02:12:24.937
And it was in exactly the same area that it was on the postmortem as on the antemortem.

02:12:25.437 --> 02:12:27.639
It was also only 5 millimeters in size.

02:12:27.639 --> 02:12:28.040
In other words,

02:12:28.040 --> 02:12:29.508
the exact same size,

02:12:30.008 --> 02:12:30.642
same area,

02:12:31.343 --> 02:12:32.377
and on both x-rays.

02:12:34.413 --> 02:12:35.948
With the positive identification,

02:12:36.481 --> 02:12:40.319
investigators have literally pulled proof of a homicide from the ashes.

02:12:41.820 --> 02:12:42.320
December

02:12:42.688 --> 02:12:46.191
21st, less than one week after Henry Campbell was reported missing,

02:12:46.892 --> 02:12:49.094
Barnes was officially charged with murder.

02:12:51.463 --> 02:12:52.331
Based on the evidence,

02:12:52.698 --> 02:12:55.934
police believe Barnes followed Campbell from his after-hours club,

02:12:56.468 --> 02:12:56.968
shot him,

02:12:57.302 --> 02:12:58.370
then hid his body.

02:13:03.375 --> 02:13:04.042
Later that night,

02:13:04.443 --> 02:13:07.279
Barnes took Campbell's body to the Animal Resource Center,

02:13:07.779 --> 02:13:08.914
where he incinerated it.

02:13:11.450 --> 02:13:13.518
In April of 1985,

02:13:13.518 --> 02:13:16.621
Clarence Barnes pled guilty to the murder of Henry Campbell.

02:13:17.556 --> 02:13:21.059
He received 15 years to life for aggravated murder.

02:13:25.230 --> 02:13:25.864
In Cleveland,

02:13:26.164 --> 02:13:28.967
police had to sift their case from a pile of ashes.

02:13:31.269 --> 02:13:31.970
In Maryland,

02:13:32.437 --> 02:13:36.341
investigators followed a winding trail of clues to capture a killer.

02:13:39.378 --> 02:13:39.845
Rockville,

02:13:39.845 --> 02:13:40.345
Maryland,

02:13:40.679 --> 02:13:41.146
August 10,

02:13:41.146 --> 02:13:44.850
1988.

02:13:44.850 --> 02:13:51.723
17-year-old Kai Lau returned early from a California vacation at the behest of a family friend to check on his mother,

02:13:52.090 --> 02:13:52.590
Lisa Tu.

02:13:55.794 --> 02:13:57.596
He hadn't heard from her in nearly a month,

02:13:58.230 --> 02:14:00.232
and others told him they hadn't heard from her either.

02:14:01.133 --> 02:14:02.367
That wasn't like his mother.

02:14:05.137 --> 02:14:08.507
When he and his mother's friend found no sign of Lisa in the house,

02:14:08.940 --> 02:14:10.142
they contacted police.

02:14:17.682 --> 02:14:22.587
They went to the Special Investigations Office at the Montgomery County Police Department to report her missing.

02:14:26.758 --> 02:14:27.559
Detective Turner?

02:14:28.326 --> 02:14:32.397
Kai informed officers that nobody had seen or heard from his mother for a month.

02:14:33.265 --> 02:14:34.533
The reason I called is...

02:14:34.633 --> 02:14:35.801
He said that his stepfather,

02:14:36.168 --> 02:14:36.835
Gregory II,

02:14:37.335 --> 02:14:38.103
might know more,

02:14:38.603 --> 02:14:41.973
but he was overseas on business and Kai didn't know how to reach him.

02:14:43.108 --> 02:14:44.142
Before Gregory left,

02:14:44.443 --> 02:14:48.847
he told friends that Lisa had flown to San Francisco to visit a friend in the hospital.

02:14:51.316 --> 02:14:56.021
Kai said it was unusual for his mother to go away without telling anyone except Gregory.

02:14:57.789 --> 02:14:59.324
Police promised to investigate.

02:15:03.962 --> 02:15:06.064
They began by contacting the airlines.

02:15:07.032 --> 02:15:11.636
Records confirmed that a one-way ticket had been purchased for LL2 on July

02:15:12.137 --> 02:15:12.637
13th.

02:15:13.171 --> 02:15:17.409
The destination was San Francisco via a connecting flight in Los Angeles.

02:15:19.311 --> 02:15:21.313
The ticket to Los Angeles had been used,

02:15:21.746 --> 02:15:23.582
but not the one for San Francisco.

02:15:26.485 --> 02:15:29.321
It appeared Lisa 2 wasn't planning to come back.

02:15:31.389 --> 02:15:32.190
That was her right,

02:15:32.657 --> 02:15:34.926
but the detectives needed to make one more call.

02:15:36.928 --> 02:15:40.465
They contacted the friend Lisa was supposed to be visiting in San Francisco.

02:15:41.466 --> 02:15:43.135
She said she hadn't been in the hospital,

02:15:43.568 --> 02:15:44.569
wasn't expecting Lisa,

02:15:44.970 --> 02:15:45.737
and hadn't talked to her.

02:15:45.770 --> 02:15:48.840
...were in months.

02:15:48.840 --> 02:15:55.380
Now detectives began to consider whether Lisa 2 intended to disappear or if there might be a more sinister explanation.

02:15:56.815 --> 02:15:58.984
Detective Roger Thompson worked the case.

02:16:00.085 --> 02:16:05.357
It was not the normal missing person case where we know somebody's missing for a particular reason.

02:16:06.024 --> 02:16:07.792
They may not contact family members,

02:16:07.826 --> 02:16:09.194
but we sooner or later find them.

02:16:09.628 --> 02:16:12.831
This had some mystery to it.

02:16:15.233 --> 02:16:16.134
To find out more,

02:16:16.535 --> 02:16:21.940
detectives spoke to several of Lisa's friends and learned the twos had been married nearly ten years.

02:16:23.441 --> 02:16:25.076
It was the second marriage for both,

02:16:25.443 --> 02:16:28.580
and Kai Lau was Lisa's son from her previous marriage.

02:16:30.282 --> 02:16:32.017
The twos had enjoyed the good life.

02:16:33.818 --> 02:16:35.754
Gregory was a successful restaurateur.

02:16:38.924 --> 02:16:39.424
In fact,

02:16:39.758 --> 02:16:42.394
one of his restaurants had been a favorite of Washington's elite.

02:16:45.330 --> 02:16:46.331
But it hadn't lasted.

02:16:47.098 --> 02:16:50.101
He'd suffered business reversals and lost his restaurants.

02:16:54.673 --> 02:16:58.376
A commissioned sales job couldn't support the family's affluent lifestyle.

02:17:01.780 --> 02:17:03.715
Gregory and Lisa argued about money,

02:17:04.316 --> 02:17:05.483
and their marriage was rocky.

02:17:09.621 --> 02:17:13.325
Police also learned that Lisa was romantically involved with another man.

02:17:15.961 --> 02:17:17.696
Police questioned him at his office.

02:17:19.431 --> 02:17:22.267
He said he spoke to Lisa the afternoon of July 14,

02:17:22.834 --> 02:17:25.237
the last day anyone saw or heard from her.

02:17:27.038 --> 02:17:33.078
He denied knowing anything about a trip to San Francisco and expressed concern over Lisa's sudden disappearance.

02:17:34.813 --> 02:17:38.083
He indicated Lisa's husband was suspicious about their relationship.

02:17:39.651 --> 02:17:41.086
When he passed a polygraph test,

02:17:41.553 --> 02:17:43.622
investigators believed he was telling the truth.

02:17:46.458 --> 02:17:51.129
Detectives checked out the family's finances and saw indications of serious problems.

02:17:53.164 --> 02:17:57.269
Gregory was $30,000 in debt.

02:17:57.335 --> 02:18:00.905
Detective Mike Turner realized too was under a lot of pressure.

02:18:03.308 --> 02:18:06.111
Gregory was known to like to gamble,

02:18:06.878 --> 02:18:08.346
and we determined that he,

02:18:08.947 --> 02:18:10.849
through interviews with friends,

02:18:11.016 --> 02:18:14.786
had accrued some debt in gambling that he had not been able to pay.

02:18:15.687 --> 02:18:23.128
And this was another problem that arose with his relationship with Lisa.

02:18:24.029 --> 02:18:29.334
Detectives also learned Lisa Tu's bank and charge accounts had been inactive since early July.

02:18:30.535 --> 02:18:32.871
That didn't make sense if she was alive.

02:18:34.572 --> 02:18:35.740
They discovered that her husband,

02:18:36.007 --> 02:18:37.909
who was still unreachable on a business trip,

02:18:38.276 --> 02:18:43.048
would collect a $200,000 life insurance policy if anything happened to his wife.

02:18:44.983 --> 02:18:51.356
Detectives began to suspect that the couple's financial woes might spell more than a simple missing persons case.

02:18:55.927 --> 02:18:57.395
As the investigation continued,

02:18:57.862 --> 02:19:00.565
Lisa's son was doing a little detective work of his own.

02:19:03.001 --> 02:19:06.004
Kai Lau found his mother's address book in her bedroom.

02:19:08.039 --> 02:19:10.975
That was odd because she seldom went anywhere without it.

02:19:13.611 --> 02:19:17.315
He also noticed that furniture in the house was missing or rearranged.

02:19:19.117 --> 02:19:20.585
He called Detective Turner.

02:19:20.852 --> 02:19:24.689
Hi,
Detective Turner.

02:19:24.689 --> 02:19:27.826
Kai returned to the police station and met with investigators.

02:19:30.028 --> 02:19:36.167
He had found that $44,000 had been withdrawn from a bank account Lisa kept for his education.

02:19:38.870 --> 02:19:39.938
And that wasn't all.

02:19:41.139 --> 02:19:45.810
Kai told police his stepfather's gun was missing and a sleep sofa had disappeared.

02:19:46.411 --> 02:19:50.115
He said his mother often slept on it when she and Gregory weren't getting along.

02:19:52.784 --> 02:19:54.986
I also informed detectives that his stepfather,

02:19:55.086 --> 02:19:55.586
Gregory,

02:19:55.820 --> 02:19:58.289
was due back from his business trip that evening.

02:19:59.958 --> 02:20:00.458
By now,

02:20:00.892 --> 02:20:03.428
investigators had a lot of questions for Gregory,

02:20:03.561 --> 02:20:04.061
too.

02:20:08.233 --> 02:20:10.935
They met him at the airport and brought him to the police station.

02:20:15.006 --> 02:20:18.176
He was concerned about his wife and was eager to help police.

02:20:21.513 --> 02:20:26.418
He told them he bought Lisa a plane ticket to San Francisco so she could visit a friend in the hospital.

02:20:28.119 --> 02:20:32.991
He said he drove her to Dulles International Airport the next day and watched her board the plane.

02:20:36.494 --> 02:20:40.832
He claimed she called from Los Angeles and said she was awaiting the flight to San Francisco.

02:20:41.566 --> 02:20:43.067
He hadn't heard from her since.

02:20:45.136 --> 02:20:45.703
When they told him

02:20:45.804 --> 02:20:48.373
Lisa's friend wasn't sick and wasn't expecting her.

02:20:48.673 --> 02:20:49.741
He seemed surprised.

02:20:52.043 --> 02:20:53.978
Then he heard another explanation.

02:20:57.015 --> 02:21:01.085
He claimed his wife was unhappy with her appearance and wanted plastic surgery.

02:21:04.255 --> 02:21:06.090
He said they'd argued about it in the past.

02:21:08.893 --> 02:21:11.763
He surmised that might have been the real reason for the trip.

02:21:15.433 --> 02:21:17.836
Lisa probably made up the story of a sick friend,

02:21:18.303 --> 02:21:21.239
realizing he wouldn't buy the ticket if he'd known the truth.

02:21:23.942 --> 02:21:26.110
When detectives inquired about the missing sofa,

02:21:26.611 --> 02:21:29.948
Two said it was infested with mice and he had it hauled away.

02:21:33.451 --> 02:21:39.691
He also said he threw the gun away after a well-publicized local incident where a homeowner shot some trespassers.

02:21:41.893 --> 02:21:46.397
He explained away the $30,000 debt as business and travel expenses.

02:21:48.700 --> 02:21:50.435
Two begged off further questions,

02:21:50.835 --> 02:21:52.770
saying he was tired from his long trip.

02:21:54.839 --> 02:21:59.310
Detectives agreed to continue the interview another day if Two would take a polygraph test.

02:22:01.279 --> 02:22:04.382
He agreed and promised to return the following Monday.

02:22:08.786 --> 02:22:11.122
Gregory Two didn't keep his appointment.

02:22:12.390 --> 02:22:13.324
He skipped town.

02:22:14.192 --> 02:22:14.859
To detectives,

02:22:15.326 --> 02:22:17.395
that seemed the act of a guilty man.

02:22:19.163 --> 02:22:24.802
We then felt that the suspicions that the family had,

02:22:25.470 --> 02:22:26.037
that we had.

02:22:26.804 --> 02:22:35.179
Put together since the initiation of the investigation now had come to fruition and we were looking probably at a homicide.

02:22:36.347 --> 02:22:37.715
Their suspicions about Gregory,

02:22:37.815 --> 02:22:38.182
too,

02:22:38.182 --> 02:22:42.253
grew even more when Kai brought them an air express letter from California.

02:22:43.388 --> 02:22:43.888
In it,

02:22:44.022 --> 02:22:46.624
Gregory claimed he was in Los Angeles looking for Lisa.

02:22:47.559 --> 02:22:48.693
If he didn't find her there,

02:22:49.060 --> 02:22:51.429
he planned to continue his search in San Francisco.

02:22:53.731 --> 02:22:57.735
Investigators considered the letter a self-serving excuse from a guilty man.

02:22:58.636 --> 02:23:02.106
But believing and proving are two different things.

02:23:03.207 --> 02:23:04.208
Without Lisa's body,

02:23:04.609 --> 02:23:06.244
there was no evidence she was dead.

02:23:07.378 --> 02:23:09.948
They began looking for proof in Gregory's story.

02:23:11.449 --> 02:23:17.522
Investigators followed up on Kai's tip about his missing college money and enlisted the help of the state's attorney's office.

02:23:18.690 --> 02:23:19.390
John McCarthy,

02:23:19.791 --> 02:23:21.726
deputy state attorney in Montgomery County,

02:23:22.060 --> 02:23:23.695
found Lisa had controlled it.

02:23:24.929 --> 02:23:26.397
Gregory II cleaned out that account.

02:23:26.864 --> 02:23:28.499
We got bank photographs from the bank,

02:23:28.600 --> 02:23:30.435
and you clear as day,

02:23:30.435 --> 02:23:33.404
you could see Gregory II stealing the $44,000 from that fund.

02:23:33.705 --> 02:23:35.807
And within a period of 10 days after he stole it,

02:23:36.040 --> 02:23:37.041
he had run through that money.

02:23:37.141 --> 02:23:37.809
It was gone.

02:23:40.778 --> 02:23:44.115
Detective Turner reexamined the airline ticket to San Francisco.

02:23:46.351 --> 02:23:48.553
They learned it was possible for someone to check in,

02:23:49.053 --> 02:23:49.954
have the ticket marked,

02:23:50.254 --> 02:23:51.589
but not board the plane.

02:23:53.825 --> 02:23:55.059
He suspected Gregory,

02:23:55.193 --> 02:23:55.560
too,

02:23:55.560 --> 02:23:58.630
had done that and that Lisa never boarded the plane.

02:24:02.166 --> 02:24:04.335
They contacted passengers who'd been on the flight.

02:24:05.136 --> 02:24:05.636
Then,

02:24:05.703 --> 02:24:07.505
Attorney McCarthy got a real break.

02:24:08.339 --> 02:24:12.343
A woman called to say she'd been on the plane with her husband and small child.

02:24:13.277 --> 02:24:14.012
While airborne,

02:24:14.379 --> 02:24:16.214
the couple took several photographs.

02:24:16.814 --> 02:24:17.248
She said,
well,

02:24:17.248 --> 02:24:19.417
I've got some pictures of the seat that Lisa,

02:24:19.484 --> 02:24:21.986
too,
was supposed to be in on that plane from

02:24:22.353 --> 02:24:23.955
Washington to San Francisco,

02:24:24.589 --> 02:24:26.190
and she's not in those pictures.

02:24:26.658 --> 02:24:28.126
And she forwarded to me those pictures,

02:24:28.259 --> 02:24:34.832
and one of the ways we were able to prove conclusively that Lisa was not on that plane that Gregory said she was on was with those pictures.

02:24:35.900 --> 02:24:38.970
Detectives wanted to know more about the missing sofa sleeper.

02:24:40.204 --> 02:24:43.841
They wondered if Lisa's body was inside it when it was hauled away.

02:24:45.543 --> 02:24:47.345
They tracked down the men who picked it up.

02:24:49.414 --> 02:24:53.451
They remembered the couch because Gregory tipped them for carrying it out to the truck.

02:24:56.354 --> 02:24:58.356
They didn't notice anything unusual about it.

02:25:00.324 --> 02:25:02.293
Investigators traced it to the landfill,

02:25:02.760 --> 02:25:03.961
but that was a dead end.

02:25:05.863 --> 02:25:08.466
The sofa had already been ground up and buried.

02:25:09.801 --> 02:25:14.906
Detectives reasoned that Gregory might have gotten rid of the sofa because it contained proof of Lisa's death.

02:25:18.710 --> 02:25:19.510
If that were true,

02:25:20.044 --> 02:25:22.080
there could be other evidence at the residence.

02:25:22.814 --> 02:25:24.782
They obtained a search warrant to find out.

02:25:26.884 --> 02:25:30.254
An examination of Gregory's car uncovered incriminating evidence.

02:25:32.790 --> 02:25:33.558
Clumps of mud,

02:25:33.991 --> 02:25:34.425
weeds,

02:25:34.425 --> 02:25:36.494
and twigs were stuck in the undercarriage.

02:25:37.061 --> 02:25:39.097
indicating the car had been driven off road.

02:25:43.000 --> 02:25:43.501
In the trunk,

02:25:43.801 --> 02:25:44.969
they found a plastic tarp,

02:25:45.369 --> 02:25:45.737
a carpet,

02:25:45.737 --> 02:25:47.004
...knife and a machete.

02:25:49.340 --> 02:25:50.108
Mike,
come over here.

02:25:51.042 --> 02:25:52.210
All of the items were clean,

02:25:52.677 --> 02:25:55.012
but they could have been used to dispose of a body.

02:26:01.586 --> 02:26:02.553
Inside the house,

02:26:02.820 --> 02:26:05.857
detectives found their first physical evidence of foul play.

02:26:07.425 --> 02:26:11.162
There were droplets of blood on a chair near the spot where the sofa had stood.

02:26:13.865 --> 02:26:16.701
But investigators suspected more might have been cleaned up.

02:26:23.541 --> 02:26:24.242
To find out,

02:26:24.542 --> 02:26:26.611
they processed the area with a luma light,

02:26:27.078 --> 02:26:30.047
which makes blood that cannot be seen with the naked eye glow.

02:26:32.884 --> 02:26:35.286
They discovered blood spatters where the sofa had been,

02:26:35.853 --> 02:26:37.622
and it displayed a significant pattern.

02:26:40.792 --> 02:26:48.966
We know that the couch was open at the time when this all occurred because there was no blood swiping or markings underneath the couch.

02:26:49.534 --> 02:26:50.501
It was a fold-away,

02:26:50.768 --> 02:26:51.502
opened-up couch,

02:26:52.036 --> 02:26:53.604
and when they put out the processing,

02:26:54.539 --> 02:26:55.173
you could see,

02:26:55.506 --> 02:26:59.143
basically see the outline of the couch opened up as a bed.

02:27:00.745 --> 02:27:03.080
Investigators also found signs of smearing,

02:27:03.748 --> 02:27:05.950
evidence someone had tried to clean up the blood.

02:27:10.054 --> 02:27:11.556
While they were processing the scene,

02:27:11.856 --> 02:27:12.690
the phone rang.

02:27:17.094 --> 02:27:20.464
A car rental agency in Las Vegas was calling for Gregory Too.

02:27:21.465 --> 02:27:24.569
The car he rented several days before was overdue.

02:27:25.469 --> 02:27:27.538
It was the break police were looking for.

02:27:28.339 --> 02:27:30.675
Now they finally knew where Too was,

02:27:31.209 --> 02:27:33.244
but they needed some help in capturing him.

02:27:34.946 --> 02:28:01.772
What I would like you to do is if he calls in about the car in the next few days or brings the car in try to stall him or if he calls in tell him that you would like to replace the car with another car because this one needs for service or whatever reason you can think of or it's going to be resold but somehow get him to come to the rental office to get another car.

02:28:06.043 --> 02:28:06.878
The plan worked.

02:28:12.650 --> 02:28:13.184
On Saturday,

02:28:13.284 --> 02:28:14.051
September 10th,

02:28:14.385 --> 02:28:16.020
he arrived at the car rental office.

02:28:16.721 --> 02:28:17.488
Yes,
your car is ready.

02:28:17.688 --> 02:28:18.188
It is?

02:28:22.426 --> 02:28:26.597
Police arrested him on a fugitive warrant nearly a month after he disappeared.

02:28:31.602 --> 02:28:34.105
Detectives Turner and Thompson flew to Las Vegas,

02:28:34.906 --> 02:28:38.676
where they began retracing Two's activities since he fled Maryland.

02:28:42.146 --> 02:28:45.616
A matchbook in his hotel room led them to a Chinese restaurant.

02:28:47.285 --> 02:28:49.754
The manager recognized Two from a photograph.

02:28:50.755 --> 02:28:53.024
She told investigators he had applied for a job,

02:28:53.624 --> 02:28:56.827
saying his wife was dead and he wanted to start a new life.

02:28:58.262 --> 02:28:59.196
On the job application,

02:28:59.563 --> 02:29:01.599
he gave his name as Greg Sen.

02:29:04.201 --> 02:29:06.337
When the detectives returned home with two,

02:29:06.837 --> 02:29:10.541
they called in a document examiner at the Maryland State Police Crime Lab.

02:29:11.809 --> 02:29:13.911
They knew Gregory had used aliases.

02:29:14.645 --> 02:29:22.920
They wanted to confirm whether the handwriting on the job application and other items he wrote matched the bank slip closing Kai Lau's college account.

02:29:23.454 --> 02:29:28.459
The stolen money would provide the motive for the more serious crime of suspected homicide.

02:29:29.860 --> 02:29:32.163
After providing known samples of Two's writing,

02:29:32.697 --> 02:29:35.366
detectives awaited the results of the document examination.

02:29:37.068 --> 02:29:38.002
The job application,

02:29:38.402 --> 02:29:38.903
bank slip,

02:29:39.170 --> 02:29:42.073
and other documents were compared against Two's handwriting.

02:29:43.207 --> 02:29:44.275
In making the comparison,

02:29:44.775 --> 02:29:46.310
factors such as writing style,

02:29:46.510 --> 02:29:47.010
speed,

02:29:47.078 --> 02:29:48.746
and pressure are all studied.

02:29:49.814 --> 02:29:52.450
Document Examiner David Sexton explains.

02:29:52.950 --> 02:30:05.396
And it's a combination of those characteristics as well as a combination of recognizable letter formations and that combination of characteristics that are unique to the individual's handwriting.

02:30:06.397 --> 02:30:08.032
The writing on the job application,

02:30:08.399 --> 02:30:08.899
bank slip,

02:30:09.166 --> 02:30:11.602
and other documents was Gregory Two's.

02:30:14.572 --> 02:30:19.977
Police also consulted a forensic serology investigator about the blood spatters found at the two house.

02:30:23.280 --> 02:30:26.584
He confirmed they were consistent with a high-velocity wound,

02:30:27.084 --> 02:30:28.619
most likely from a gunshot.

02:30:30.021 --> 02:30:32.423
Despite all the evidence investigators had collected,

02:30:32.823 --> 02:30:34.325
their case was still flawed.

02:30:35.092 --> 02:30:37.461
They needed something solid to prove murder.

02:30:40.164 --> 02:30:41.265
Without Lisa's body,

02:30:41.699 --> 02:30:44.802
the only physical evidence they had was blood from the house.

02:30:46.804 --> 02:30:48.839
They had to somehow tie it to the victim.

02:30:51.442 --> 02:30:52.843
They had only one chance,

02:30:53.377 --> 02:30:54.678
and it was a long shot.

02:30:59.016 --> 02:31:01.018
To prove Lisa 2 had been murdered,

02:31:01.419 --> 02:31:04.789
Maryland investigators had to link her to the blood in the house.

02:31:05.656 --> 02:31:08.192
They hoped forensic DNA analysis could do that,

02:31:08.793 --> 02:31:10.261
but in 1988,

02:31:10.261 --> 02:31:11.395
it was still in its infancy.

02:31:13.064 --> 02:31:13.564
In fact,

02:31:13.898 --> 02:31:17.101
DNA evidence had only been used in a few trials nationally,

02:31:17.601 --> 02:31:19.070
none of them murder cases.

02:31:20.271 --> 02:31:22.606
And the 2 case posed a significant challenge.

02:31:23.407 --> 02:31:28.145
There were no known samples of Lisa 2's DNA that could be used for comparison.

02:31:30.114 --> 02:31:30.614
Without them,

02:31:30.915 --> 02:31:33.918
it would be difficult to connect Lisa with the blood spatter.

02:31:37.688 --> 02:31:41.092
The DNA lab needed to perform a reverse paternity test,

02:31:41.625 --> 02:31:43.761
but this time they would have to work backward.

02:31:46.263 --> 02:31:49.433
They would determine Lisa's DNA from the blood of her son.

02:31:55.039 --> 02:31:58.409
Because Kai Lau was a genetic combination of both his parents,

02:31:58.943 --> 02:31:59.810
scientists could,

02:31:59.844 --> 02:32:00.344
in theory,

02:32:00.811 --> 02:32:04.014
forensically subtract his father's DNA from his blood.

02:32:05.883 --> 02:32:08.819
What remained would be Lisa's DNA profile.

02:32:10.855 --> 02:32:15.326
Blood was drawn from Kai Lau and from his father in Hong Kong for comparison.

02:32:16.393 --> 02:32:17.661
By process of elimination,

02:32:17.962 --> 02:32:18.963
the lab confirmed,

02:32:19.330 --> 02:32:21.765
with 98 to 99 percent accuracy,

02:32:22.199 --> 02:32:25.035
that blood found at the Tu house was Lisa's.

02:32:26.904 --> 02:32:28.539
With forensic DNA testing,

02:32:28.973 --> 02:32:31.342
detectives were able to prove homicide.

02:32:33.577 --> 02:32:35.012
Investigators believe Gregory,

02:32:35.146 --> 02:32:35.479
too,

02:32:35.479 --> 02:32:42.753
was moved to murder by jealousy over his wife's affair and the prospect of solving his financial problems with her life insurance.

02:32:44.355 --> 02:32:47.291
They pieced together a likely scenario for the night of July

02:32:47.625 --> 02:32:48.125
14th.

02:32:50.094 --> 02:32:52.196
There were indications that the couple had argued,

02:32:52.763 --> 02:32:53.831
perhaps over the affair,

02:32:54.365 --> 02:32:55.332
perhaps about money.

02:32:56.667 --> 02:32:57.902
Desperate and enraged,

02:32:58.269 --> 02:33:02.406
Gregory II pulled his gun from the cabinet and shot Lisa.

02:33:06.844 --> 02:33:08.479
He then disposed of her body.

02:33:11.882 --> 02:33:13.684
Despite his protestations of innocence,

02:33:14.218 --> 02:33:17.721
Gregory II was convicted of first-degree murder on November 21,

02:33:17.721 --> 02:33:19.323
1989.

02:33:20.057 --> 02:33:22.092
He is currently serving a life sentence.

02:33:22.993 --> 02:33:25.396
Lisa II's body has never been found.

02:33:27.031 --> 02:33:27.531
Once,

02:33:27.698 --> 02:33:30.801
police had no chance of proving murder without a body.

02:33:31.669 --> 02:33:32.203
But today,

02:33:32.603 --> 02:33:35.072
they can make a case on forensic science,

02:33:35.706 --> 02:33:39.043
even when the victim has disappeared without a trace.

